  TR["VN-H069"] = {
    "lawId": "VN-H069",
    "sourceLang": "zh",
    "targetLang": "en",
    "engine": "llm",
    "model": "deepseek-v4.1-flash",
    "promptVersion": "calr-vi2en-v1",
    "translatedAt": "2026-10-28",
    "reviewed": "machine",
    "completenessNote": "逐条对译，共 43 条；条号与原文一一对应，未合并、未省略。",
    "articles": [
      {
        "no": 1,
        "text": {
          "en": "·39· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam Pursuant to the Constitution of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam, the National Assembly promulgates the Law on Cybersecurity.\nChapter I GENERAL PROVISIONS\nScope of regulation This Law provides for activities to safeguard national security and ensure social order and safety in cyberspace, and for the responsibilities of the agencies, organisations and individuals concerned."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 1"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 2,
        "text": {
          "en": "Interpretation of terms In this Law, the following terms shall be construed as follows:\n1. Cybersecurity means the assurance that activities in cyberspace do not harm national security, social order and safety, or the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals.\n2. Cybersecurity protection means the prevention, detection, stoppage and handling of acts infringing cybersecurity.\n3. Cyberspace means the connecting network of information technology infrastructure, including telecommunications networks, the Internet, computer networks, information systems, information processing and control systems and databases; it is the place where people carry out social acts without being limited by space and time.\n4. National cyberspace means cyberspace established, managed and supervised by the Government.\n5. National cyberspace infrastructure means the system of physical and technical facilities for creating, transmitting, collecting, processing, storing and exchanging information in national cyberspace, comprising:\n(a) Transmission systems, including domestic transmission systems, international connection transmission systems, satellite systems and the transmission systems of providers of telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services in cyberspace.\n(b) Core service systems, including the national information routing and navigation system, the national domain name resolution system (DNS), the national authentication system (PKI/CA) and the Internet connection and access service provision systems of providers of telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services in cyberspace.\n(c) Information technology services and applications, including online services, information technology applications for connection with the management and dispatching service networks of important agencies, organisations and economic and financial groups, * Translator: Dao Wenwen, doctoral student at the School of Asian and African Studies, Beijing Foreign Studies University.\n* Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·40· and national databases.\nOnline services include e-government, e-commerce, websites, online forums, social networks and blogs.\n(d) Information technology infrastructure of smart cities, the Internet of Things, virtual systems, cloud computing, big data systems, fast data systems and artificial intelligence systems.\n6. International network connection port means the place at which network signals are transmitted and received between Vietnam and other countries and territories.\n7. Cybercrime means the use of cyberspace, information technology or electronic means to commit the crimes provided for in the Criminal Code.\n8. Cyberattack means the use of cyberspace, information technology or electronic means to damage or interrupt the operation of telecommunications networks, the Internet, computer networks, information systems, information processing and control systems, databases and electronic means.\n9. Cyberterrorism means the use of cyberspace, information technology or electronic means to carry out terrorism or the financing of terrorism.\n10. Cyber espionage means the deliberate circumvention of warnings, access codes, passwords and firewalls, the use of the administrative rights of others or other methods in order to unlawfully appropriate or collect information and information resources on telecommunications networks, the Internet, computer networks, information systems, information management and control systems, databases and electronic means of agencies, organisations and individuals.\n11. Digital account means the information used to authenticate, verify and authorise the use of applications and services in cyberspace.\n12. Cybersecurity threat means a situation in which signals of a threat infringing national security, seriously harming social order and safety or the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals appear in cyberspace.\n13. Cybersecurity incident means an unexpected event occurring in cyberspace that infringes national security, social order and safety or the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals.\n14. Dangerous cybersecurity situation means an event occurring in cyberspace that seriously infringes national security and causes particularly serious harm to social order and safety or to the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 2"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 3,
        "text": {
          "en": "State policy on cybersecurity\n1. To give priority to protecting the security of national defence, security, socio-economic development, science and technology and foreign affairs networks.\n2. To build a healthy cyberspace that does not harm national security or social order and safety and does not damage the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals.\n3. To give priority to building a specialised cybersecurity protection force; to strengthen the cybersecurity protection forces and enhance the capacity of organisations and individuals to participate in cybersecurity protection; to give priority to investment in scientific and technological research and development in order to safeguard cybersecurity.\n4. To encourage organisations and individuals to participate in cybersecurity protection and in handling cybersecurity threats, and to create conditions for them to do so; to research and develop cybersecurity protection technologies, products, services and applications; and to coordinate with the functional agencies in cybersecurity protection.\n5. To strengthen international cooperation on cybersecurity.\n6. ·41· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 3"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 4,
        "text": {
          "en": "Principles of cybersecurity protection\n1. To comply with the Constitution and the law and to safeguard national interests and the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals.\n2. To be under the leadership of the Communist Party of Vietnam and the unified management of the State, to mobilise the combined strength of the political system and of the whole people, and to bring into play the core role of the specialised cybersecurity protection force.\n3. To closely combine the task of protecting cybersecurity and information systems important to national security with the tasks of socio-economic development, safeguarding human and citizens' rights and creating conditions for the activities of agencies, organisations and individuals in cyberspace.\n4. To actively prevent, detect, stop, combat and block all activities that use cyberspace to infringe national security, social order and safety or the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals, and to be ready at all times to deal with cybersecurity threats.\n5. To carry out cybersecurity protection activities for the national cyberspace infrastructure and to adopt measures to protect information systems important to national security.\n6. Information systems important to national security shall, before being put into operation and use, be appraised and certified as satisfying cybersecurity conditions; in the course of use, cybersecurity shall be regularly inspected and supervised and cybersecurity incidents shall be promptly responded to and resolved.\n7. All acts in violation of the law on cybersecurity must be promptly and strictly handled."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 4"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 5,
        "text": {
          "en": "Measures for cybersecurity protection\n1. Measures for cybersecurity protection comprise:\n(a) Cybersecurity assessment.\n(b) Assessment of cybersecurity conditions.\n(c) Cybersecurity inspection.\n(d) Cybersecurity monitoring.\n(dd) Response to and resolution of cybersecurity incidents.\n(e) Cybersecurity protection combat.\n(g) Blocking and requesting the suspension or cessation of the provision of online information, and the cessation or suspension of the establishment, provision and use of telecommunications networks and the Internet and of the production and use of radio transmitters and transceivers in accordance with law.\n(h) Requesting the deletion of, or access to the deletion of, unlawful or false information in cyberspace that infringes national security, social order and safety or the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals.\n(i) Collecting electronic data relating to activities infringing national security, social order and safety or the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals in cyberspace.\n(k) Blocking or restricting the operation of information systems; ceasing or suspending the operation of information systems in accordance with law, or requesting an information system to cease operation or revoking a domain name.\n(l) Initiating proceedings, investigating, prosecuting and adjudicating in accordance with the Criminal Procedure Code.\n(m) Other measures provided for by the Law on National Security and the Law on Handling of Administrative Violations.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·42·\n2. The procedures and formalities for applying cybersecurity protection measures shall be provided for by the Government, except for the measures provided for in items (l) and\n(m) of paragraph 1 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 5"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 6,
        "text": {
          "en": "Protection of national cyberspace The State shall adopt measures to protect national cyberspace and to prevent and handle acts that infringe national security, social order and safety or the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals in cyberspace."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 6"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 7,
        "text": {
          "en": "International cooperation on cybersecurity\n1. International cooperation on cybersecurity shall be carried out on the basis of respect for independence, sovereignty and territorial integrity, non-interference in each other's internal affairs, equality and mutual benefit.\n2. The contents of international cooperation on cybersecurity comprise:\n(a) Researching and analysing cybersecurity trends;\n(b) Establishing mechanisms and policies to promote cooperation between Vietnamese organisations and individuals and foreign organisations and individuals and international organisations in cybersecurity activities;\n(c) Sharing information and experience and providing training, equipment and technical support for cybersecurity protection;\n(d) Preventing and combating cybercrime and acts infringing cybersecurity and preventing cybersecurity threats;\n(dd) Consultancy, training and development of cybersecurity human resources;\n(e) Organising international conferences, seminars and forums on cybersecurity;\n(g) Signing and implementing international treaties and international agreements on cybersecurity;\n(h) Implementing international cooperation projects and programmes on cybersecurity;\n(i) Other international cooperation activities relating to cybersecurity.\n3. The Ministry of Public Security shall be accountable to the Government and shall take the lead, in coordination with other agencies, in carrying out international cooperation on cybersecurity, except for the international cooperation activities of the Ministry of National Defence.\nThe Ministry of National Defence shall be accountable to the Government and shall carry out international cooperation on cybersecurity within the scope of its management.\nThe Ministry of Foreign Affairs is responsible for coordinating with the Ministry of Public Security and the Ministry of National Defence in international cooperation activities on cybersecurity.\nCases of international cooperation on cybersecurity involving the responsibilities of several ministries shall be decided by the Government.\n4. Other ministries and localities must, before carrying out international cooperation activities on cybersecurity, obtain the written opinion of participation of the Ministry of Public Security, except for the international cooperation activities of the Ministry of National Defence."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 7"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 8,
        "text": {
          "en": "Prohibited acts relating to cybersecurity\n1. It is prohibited to use cyberspace for the following acts:\n(a) The acts provided for in paragraph 1 of Article 18 of this Law;\n(b) Organising, operating, colluding, instigating, bribing, deceiving, luring or training persons to oppose the Socialist Republic of Vietnam;\n·43· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam\n(c) Distorting history, denying revolutionary achievements, undermining great national unity, offending religion, or discriminating on grounds of gender and ethnicity;\n(d) Publishing false information that causes panic among the people, harms socio-economic activities, obstructs the activities of State agencies or public employees, or infringes the lawful rights and interests of other agencies, organisations and individuals;\n(dd) Engaging in prostitution, social evils and human trafficking, or publishing obscene or decadent information or information about crimes, thereby damaging the fine customs and traditions, social morality and social health;\n(e) Instigating, luring or inciting others to commit crimes.\n2. Conducting cyberattacks, cyberterrorism, cyber espionage or cybercrime that causes incidents, interruption, stagnation, paralysis, failure or the seizure of control, intrusion into or destruction of information systems important to national security.\n3. Producing or putting into use tools, means or software, or using acts, to obstruct or disrupt the operation of telecommunications networks, the Internet, computer networks, information systems, information processing and control systems and electronic means; disseminating computer programmes that harm the operation of telecommunications networks, the Internet, computer networks, information systems, information processing and control systems and electronic means; or unlawfully intruding into the telecommunications networks, computer networks, information systems, information processing and control systems, databases and electronic means of others.\n4. Opposing or obstructing the activities of the specialised cybersecurity protection force, or unlawfully attacking or disabling cybersecurity protection measures so as to render them ineffective.\n5. Using or abusing cybersecurity protection activities to infringe national sovereignty, interests and security, social order and safety, or the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals.\n6. Other acts in violation of this Law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 8"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 9,
        "text": {
          "en": "Handling of violations of the law on cybersecurity Any person committing an act in violation of this Law shall, depending on the nature and seriousness of the violation, be subject to disciplinary action, administrative penalty or criminal liability in accordance with law, and shall make compensation in accordance with law where loss is caused.\nChapter II CYBERSECURITY PROTECTION OF INFORMATION SYSTEMS IMPORTANT TO NATIONAL SECURITY"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 9"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 10,
        "text": {
          "en": "Information systems important to national security\n1. Information systems important to national security mean information systems which, in the event of an incident, intrusion, seizure of control, failure, interruption, stoppage, paralysis, attack or destruction, would seriously infringe cybersecurity.\n2. Information systems important to national security comprise:\n(a) Military, security, diplomatic and cipher information systems;\n(b) Information systems storing and processing State secret information;\n(c) Information systems used to store and preserve physical objects and materials of particularly important value;\n(d) Information systems used to preserve materials and substances particularly dangerous to human beings and the ecological environment;\n(dd) Information systems used to preserve, produce and manage the material foundations particularly important to national security;\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·44·\n(e) Important information systems serving the activities of central agencies and organisations;\n(g) National information systems in the fields of energy, finance, banking, telecommunications, transport, natural resources and environment, chemicals, health care, culture and the press;\n(h) Automatic control and monitoring systems in works important to national security and in targets important to national security.\n3. The Prime Minister shall issue, amend and supplement the list of information systems important to national security.\n4. The Government shall provide for the coordination among the Ministry of Public Security, the Ministry of National Defence, the Ministry of Information and Communications, the Government Cipher Department and the functional ministries in the appraisal, assessment, inspection and supervision of, and response to and resolution of incidents in, information systems important to national security."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 10"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 11,
        "text": {
          "en": "Cybersecurity appraisal of information systems important to national security\n1. Cybersecurity appraisal means the activity of examining and assessing matters relating to cybersecurity in order to provide a basis for decisions on the establishment or upgrading of an information system.\n2. The objects of cybersecurity appraisal of information systems important to national security comprise:\n(a) The pre-feasibility study report and the design and construction dossier, before approval of an investment project for the construction of an information system;\n(b) An information system upgrading project, before approval.\n3. The contents of cybersecurity appraisal of information systems important to national security comprise:\n(a) Compliance of the design with the legal provisions and conditions on cybersecurity;\n(b) Conformity with the plans for protection, response and resolution of incidents, and the staffing arrangements for cybersecurity protection.\n4. The competence to appraise the cybersecurity of information systems important to national security is provided for as follows:\n(a) The specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security shall appraise the cybersecurity of information systems important to national security, except in the cases provided for in items (b) and (c) of this paragraph;\n(b) The specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of National Defence shall appraise the cybersecurity of military information systems;\n(c) The Government Cipher Department shall appraise the cybersecurity of the cipher information systems under its management."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 11"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 12,
        "text": {
          "en": "Assessment of cybersecurity conditions of information systems important to national security\n1. Assessment of cybersecurity conditions means the activity of examining whether an information system satisfies cybersecurity conditions before it is put into operation and use.\n2. An information system important to national security shall satisfy the following conditions:\n(a) It has regulations, procedures and plans for cybersecurity protection and personnel for the operation and management of the system;\n(b) It is able to ensure the cybersecurity of the equipment, hardware and software constituting the system components;\n(c) It has technical measures for monitoring and protecting cybersecurity and measures for protecting automatic control and monitoring systems, the Internet of Things, virtual systems, cloud computing, big data systems, fast data systems and artificial intelligence systems;\n(d) It has measures for protecting physical security, including special isolation, prevention of data leakage, prevention of reception and control of entry and exit.\n·45· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam\n3. The competence to assess the cybersecurity conditions of information systems important to national security is provided for as follows:\n(a) The specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security shall assess and certify the cybersecurity conditions of information systems important to national security, except in the cases provided for in items (b) and (c) of this paragraph;\n(b) The specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of National Defence shall assess and certify the cybersecurity conditions of military information systems;\n(c) The Government Cipher Department shall assess and certify the cybersecurity conditions of the cipher information systems under its management.\n4. An information system important to national security may be put into operation and use only after it has been certified as satisfying cybersecurity conditions.\n5. The Government shall provide specifically for paragraph 2 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 12"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 13,
        "text": {
          "en": "Cybersecurity inspection of information systems important to national security\n1. Cybersecurity inspection means the activity of determining the current cybersecurity status of an information system, of the infrastructure of an information system or of the information stored, processed and transmitted in an information system, in order to prevent, detect and handle cybersecurity threats and to propose plans and measures for ensuring the normal operation of the information system.\n2. Cybersecurity inspection of information systems important to national security shall be carried out in the following cases:\n(a) When electronic means and network information security services are put into use in the information system;\n(b) When there is a change in the current status of the information system;\n(c) Periodic annual inspection;\n(d) Ad hoc inspection upon the occurrence of a cybersecurity incident or an act infringing cybersecurity, at the request of the State management of cybersecurity, or upon expiry of the period set by the specialised cybersecurity protection force for remedying confidentiality vulnerabilities and gaps.\n3. The objects of cybersecurity inspection of information systems important to national security comprise:\n(a) The system of hardware, software and digital devices used in the information system;\n(b) The regulations and measures for cybersecurity protection;\n(c) The information stored, processed and transmitted in the information system;\n(d) The plans for responding to and resolving cybersecurity incidents of the person in charge of the information system;\n(dd) The measures for protecting State secrets and for preventing and combating the disclosure and loss of State secrets through technical channels;\n(e) The personnel engaged in cybersecurity protection.\n4. In the cases provided for in items (a), (b) and (c) of paragraph 2 of this Article, the person in charge of an information system important to national security is responsible for inspecting the cybersecurity of the information system under his or her management, and shall notify the results of the inspection in writing to the specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security, or notify the results of the inspection of military information systems to the specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of National Defence, before October each year.\n5. Ad hoc cybersecurity inspection of information systems important to national security is provided for as follows:\n(a) Where a cybersecurity incident or an act infringing cybersecurity has occurred before the inspection, the specialised cybersecurity protection force shall notify the person in charge of the information system in writing at least 12 hours in advance; where the State management of cybersecurity so requires, or upon expiry of the period set by the specialised cybersecurity Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·46· protection force for remedying confidentiality vulnerabilities and gaps, it shall notify the person in charge of the information system in writing at least 72 hours in advance.\n(b) The specialised cybersecurity protection force shall notify the results of the inspection within 30 days from the date of completion of the inspection; where confidentiality vulnerabilities or gaps are found, it shall make requests to the person in charge of the information system, or shall provide guidance or participate in remedying them at the proposal of the person in charge of the information system.\n(c) The specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security shall carry out ad hoc inspection of information systems important to national security, except for military information systems managed by the Ministry of National Defence and cipher information systems under the Government Cipher Department and the cipher products it supplies for the protection of State secrets.\nThe specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of National Defence shall carry out ad hoc cybersecurity inspection of military information systems.\nThe Government Cipher Department shall carry out ad hoc cybersecurity inspection of the cipher information systems under its management and of the cipher products it supplies for the protection of State secrets.\n(d) The person in charge of an information system important to national security is responsible for coordinating with the specialised cybersecurity protection force in ad hoc cybersecurity inspection.\n6. The results of cybersecurity inspection shall be kept confidential in accordance with law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 13"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 14,
        "text": {
          "en": "Cybersecurity supervision of information systems important to national security\n1. Cybersecurity supervision means the activity of collecting and analysing information and identifying cybersecurity threats, cybersecurity incidents, confidentiality vulnerabilities and gaps, malicious code and malicious hardware in order to give warnings and to resolve and handle them.\n2. The person in charge of an information system important to national security shall take the lead, in coordination with the competent specialised cybersecurity protection force, in carrying out regular cybersecurity supervision of the information system within the scope of management; shall establish a mechanism for self-warning and for receiving warnings concerning cybersecurity threats, cybersecurity incidents, confidentiality vulnerabilities and gaps, malicious code and malicious hardware; and shall propose emergency response and resolution plans.\n3. The specialised cybersecurity protection force shall carry out cybersecurity supervision of the information systems important to national security under its management, shall give warnings concerning cybersecurity threats, cybersecurity incidents, confidentiality vulnerabilities and gaps, malicious code and malicious hardware occurring in information systems important to national security, and shall coordinate with the person in charge of the information system in resolving and handling the risks."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 14"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 15,
        "text": {
          "en": "Response to and resolution of cybersecurity incidents in information systems important to national security\n1. Activities for responding to and resolving cybersecurity incidents in information systems important to national security comprise:\n(a) Detecting and identifying the cybersecurity incident;\n(b) Protecting the scene and collecting evidence;\n(c) Blocking and controlling the scope of the cybersecurity incident and limiting the loss caused by it;\n(d) Determining the objectives, objects and scope of the emergency response;\n(dd) Verifying, analysing, assessing and classifying the cybersecurity incident;\n(e) Formulating plans for responding to and resolving the cybersecurity incident;\n(g) Verifying the causes and tracing the origin;\n·47· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam\n(h) Investigating and handling in accordance with law.\n2. The person in charge of an information system important to national security shall formulate plans for responding to and resolving cybersecurity incidents for the information system under his or her management, shall implement the plans when a cybersecurity incident occurs and shall promptly report to the competent specialised cybersecurity protection force.\n3. The response to and resolution of cybersecurity incidents in information systems important to national security shall be coordinated as follows:\n(a) The specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security shall take the lead and coordinate the response to and resolution of cybersecurity incidents in information systems important to national security, except in the cases provided for in items (b) and (c) of this paragraph; it shall participate in the response to and resolution of cybersecurity incidents in information systems important to national security when so requested, and shall notify the person in charge of the information system when a cyberattack or cybersecurity incident is detected.\n(b) The specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of National Defence shall take the lead and coordinate the response to and resolution of cybersecurity incidents in military information systems.\n(c) The Government Cipher Department shall take the lead and coordinate the response to and resolution of cybersecurity incidents in the cipher information systems under its management.\n4. Agencies, organisations and individuals are responsible for participating, at the request of the lead and coordinating unit, in the response to and resolution of cybersecurity incidents in information systems important to national security.\nChapter III PREVENTION AND HANDLING OF ACTS INFRINGING CYBERSECURITY"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 15"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 16,
        "text": {
          "en": "Prevention and handling of information in cyberspace containing propaganda against the Socialist Republic of Vietnam, incitement to disturbances, undermining of security, disturbance of public order, insult or slander, or content violating the order of economic management\n1. Information in cyberspace containing propaganda against the Socialist Republic of Vietnam comprises:\n(a) Propaganda distorting or slandering the people's administration;\n(b) Psychological warfare inciting wars of aggression, division and hatred among ethnic groups, religions and the peoples of various countries;\n(c) Offending the nation, the national flag, the national emblem, the national anthem, great persons, leaders, famous persons and national heroes.\n2. Information in cyberspace containing incitement to disturbances, undermining of security or disturbance of public order comprises:\n(a) Calls, mobilisation, instigation, threats, division, armed activities or the use of force against the people's administration;\n(b) Calls, mobilisation, instigation or threats, or luring of the masses into gathering to cause disturbances, opposing public employees, obstructing the activities of agencies and organisations or causing instability of order and safety.\n3. Information in cyberspace containing insulting or slanderous content comprises:\n(a) Information seriously infringing the honour, reputation and dignity of others;\n(b) Forged or false information infringing the honour, reputation or dignity of other agencies, organisations and individuals or damaging their lawful rights and interests.\n4. Information in cyberspace containing content violating the order of economic management comprises:\n(a) Forged or false Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·48· information about products, goods, currency, bonds, securities, public debt, cheques and other valuable papers;\n(b) Forged or false information in the fields of finance, banking, e-commerce, electronic payment, currency trading, fund-raising, multi-level marketing and securities.\n5. Information in cyberspace that causes panic among the people, harms socio-economic activities, obstructs the activities of State agencies or public employees, or infringes the lawful rights and interests of other agencies, organisations and individuals.\n6. The person in charge of an information system is responsible, at the request of the specialised cybersecurity protection force, for adopting managerial and technical measures to prevent, detect, block and delete information containing the contents provided for in paragraphs 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 of this Article within the scope of his or her management.\n7. The specialised cybersecurity protection force and the competent agencies shall adopt the measures provided for in items (g), (h) and (k) of paragraph 1 of Article 5 of this Law to handle information in cyberspace containing the contents provided for in paragraphs 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 of this Article.\n8. Providers of telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services in cyberspace and persons in charge of information systems are responsible for coordinating with the functional agencies in handling information in cyberspace containing the contents provided for in paragraphs 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 of this Article.\n9. Organisations and individuals that compile, publish or disseminate in cyberspace information containing the contents provided for in paragraphs 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 of this Article shall remove the information at the request of the specialised cybersecurity protection force and shall bear responsibility in accordance with law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 16"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 17,
        "text": {
          "en": "Prevention and combating of cyber espionage and protection of information in cyberspace constituting State secrets, work secrets, business secrets, personal privacy and the privacy of family and private life\n1. Cyber espionage means acts infringing State secrets, work secrets, business secrets, personal privacy and the privacy of family and private life in cyberspace, comprising:\n(a) Appropriating, trading in, withholding or deliberately disclosing information constituting State secrets, work secrets, business secrets, personal privacy or the privacy of family and private life, thereby affecting the honour, reputation, dignity and lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals;\n(b) Deliberately deleting, damaging, losing or altering information constituting State secrets, work secrets, business secrets, personal privacy or the privacy of family and private life that is transmitted and stored in cyberspace;\n(c) Deliberately altering, cancelling or deactivating the technical measures formulated and used to protect information constituting State secrets, work secrets, business secrets, personal privacy or the privacy of family and private life;\n(d) Uploading information constituting State secrets, work secrets, business secrets, personal privacy or the privacy of family and private life to cyberspace in violation of legal provisions;\n(dd) Deliberately wiretapping, audio-recording or video-recording the content of conversations unlawfully;\n(e) Other acts deliberately infringing State secrets, work secrets, business secrets, personal privacy or the privacy of family and private life.\n·49· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam\n2. The person in charge of an information system has the following responsibilities:\n(a) To inspect cybersecurity, detect and remove malicious code and malicious hardware, remedy confidentiality vulnerabilities and gaps, and detect, block and handle unlawful intrusion activities or other threats to cybersecurity.\n(b) To carry out managerial and technical measures to prevent, detect and stop cyber espionage and acts infringing State secrets, work secrets, business secrets, personal privacy or the privacy of family and private life on the information system, and to promptly delete information relating to such acts.\n(c) To coordinate with, and comply with the requirements of, the specialised cybersecurity protection force in the prevention and combating of cyber espionage and the protection of information constituting State secrets, work secrets, business secrets, personal privacy and the privacy of family and private life on information systems.\n3. Agencies that compile and store State secret information and documents are responsible, in accordance with the law on the protection of State secrets, for protecting State secrets compiled or stored on computers and other devices or exchanged in cyberspace.\n4. Apart from the provisions of paragraphs 5 and 6 of this Article, the Ministry of Public Security has the following responsibilities:\n(a) To carry out cybersecurity inspection of information systems important to national security, detect and remove malicious code and malicious hardware, remedy confidentiality vulnerabilities and gaps, and detect, block and handle unlawful intrusion activities;\n(b) To carry out cybersecurity inspection of communication equipment, products, services and digital and electronic devices before they are put into use in information systems important to national security;\n(c) To carry out cybersecurity supervision of information systems important to national security and to detect and handle activities of unlawfully collecting State secret information;\n(d) To detect and handle acts of unlawfully publishing, storing and exchanging State secret information and documents in cyberspace;\n(dd) To participate, in accordance with its functions and assigned tasks, in researching and producing products for storing and transmitting State secret information and documents and products for encoding information in cyberspace;\n(e) To supervise and inspect the protection of State secrets in cyberspace by State agencies and the cybersecurity protection work of persons in charge of information systems important to national security;\n(g) To organise training and drills, in accordance with paragraph 2 of Article 30 of this Law, in order to raise the awareness and knowledge of the specialised cybersecurity protection force concerning the protection of State secrets in cyberspace, the prevention and combating of cyberattacks and the protection of cybersecurity.\n5. The Ministry of National Defence is responsible for implementing the contents provided for in items (a), (b), (c), (d), (dd) and (e) of paragraph 4 of this Article in respect of military information systems.\n6. The Government Cipher Department is responsible for organising the implementation of legal provisions on the cryptographic protection of State secret information stored and transmitted in cyberspace."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 17"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 18,
        "text": {
          "en": "Prevention and combating of acts using cyberspace, information technology and electronic means to violate the law on national security and social order and safety\n1. Acts using cyberspace, information technology and electronic means to violate the law on national security and social order and safety comprise:\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·50·\n(a) Publishing or disseminating in cyberspace information containing the contents provided for in paragraphs 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 of Article 16 and the acts provided for in Article 17 of this Law;\n(b) Appropriating property in cyberspace, organising or participating in gambling through the Internet, stealing international telecommunications charges of the Internet, or infringing copyright and intellectual property rights in cyberspace;\n(c) Forging the websites of agencies, organisations and individuals; unlawfully forging, circulating, stealing, trading in or collecting or exchanging information on the credit cards and bank accounts of others; or unlawfully issuing, providing or using means of payment;\n(d) Propagating, promoting or selling goods and services prohibited by law;\n(dd) Guiding others to commit unlawful acts;\n(e) Other acts using cyberspace, information technology or electronic means to violate the law on national security and social order and safety.\n2. The specialised cybersecurity protection force is responsible for preventing and combating acts using cyberspace, information technology and electronic means to violate the law on national security and social order and safety."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 18"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 19,
        "text": {
          "en": "Prevention and combating of cyberattacks\n1. Cyberattacks and related acts comprise:\n(a) Disseminating computer programmes that harm telecommunications networks, the Internet, computer networks, information systems, information processing and control systems, databases and electronic means;\n(b) Unlawfully obstructing, disrupting, interrupting, terminating or blocking the transmission of data in telecommunications networks, the Internet, computer networks, information systems, information processing and control systems and electronic means, causing them to fail;\n(c) Intruding into, destroying or appropriating data stored and transmitted in telecommunications networks, the Internet, computer networks, information processing and control systems, databases and electronic means;\n(d) Intruding into, creating or exploiting confidentiality vulnerabilities, gaps and system services in order to appropriate information or obtain unlawful benefits;\n(dd) Producing, trading in, exchanging or giving away tools, equipment and software capable of conducting cyberattacks on telecommunications networks, the Internet, computer networks, information systems, information processing and control systems, databases and electronic means, for unlawful purposes;\n(e) Other acts affecting the normal operation of telecommunications networks, the Internet, computer networks, information systems, information processing and control systems, databases and electronic means.\n2. The person in charge of an information system is responsible for adopting technical measures for the information system under his or her management to prevent and block the acts provided for in items (a), (b), (c), (d) and (e) of paragraph 1 of this Article.\n3. When a cyberattack occurs that infringes or threatens to infringe national sovereignty, interests and security, or seriously damages social order and safety, the specialised cybersecurity protection force shall take the lead, in coordination with the person in charge of the information system and the organisations and individuals concerned, in adopting measures to identify the origin of the cyberattack and collect evidence; it shall request providers of telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services in cyberspace to intercept and filter information, to stop and eliminate the cyberattack, and to provide relevant information and documents fully and promptly.\n·51· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam\n4. Responsibility for preventing and combating cyberattacks is provided for as follows:\n(a) The Ministry of Public Security shall take the lead, in coordination with the relevant ministries, in preventing, detecting and handling the acts provided for in paragraph 1 of this Article that infringe or threaten to infringe national sovereignty, interests and security or cause serious harm to social order and safety throughout the country, except in the cases provided for in items (b) and\n(c) of this paragraph;\n(b) The Ministry of National Defence shall take the lead, in coordination with the relevant ministries, in preventing, detecting and handling the acts provided for in paragraph 1 of this Article in respect of military information systems;\n(c) The Government Cipher Department shall take the lead, in coordination with the relevant ministries, in preventing, detecting and handling the acts provided for in paragraph 1 of this Article in respect of the information systems of the Government Cipher Department."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 19"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 20,
        "text": {
          "en": "Prevention and combating of cyberterrorism\n1. The competent State agencies are responsible for adopting measures to handle cyberterrorism in accordance with this Law, Article 29 of the Law on Network Information Security and the law on counter-terrorism.\n2. The person in charge of an information system shall regularly examine and inspect the information system under his or her management in order to eliminate threats of cyberterrorism.\n3. Agencies, organisations and individuals that detect signs and acts of cyberterrorism shall promptly notify the specialised cybersecurity protection force. The agency receiving the notification is responsible for receiving fully the information on cyberterrorism and for promptly notifying the specialised cybersecurity protection force.\n4. The Ministry of Public Security shall take the lead, in coordination with the relevant ministries, in preventing and combating cyberterrorism, adopting measures to eliminate the sources of cyberterrorism, handling cyberterrorism and minimising its consequences for information systems, except in the cases provided for in paragraphs 5 and 6 of this Article.\n5. The Ministry of National Defence shall take the lead, in coordination with the relevant ministries, in preventing and combating cyberterrorism and adopting measures to handle cyberterrorism directed against military information systems.\n6. The Government Cipher Department shall take the lead, in coordination with the relevant ministries, in preventing and combating cyberterrorism and adopting measures to handle cyberterrorism directed against the cipher information systems under its management."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 20"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 21,
        "text": {
          "en": "Prevention and handling of dangerous cybersecurity situations\n1. Dangerous cybersecurity situations comprise:\n(a) The appearance in cyberspace of inciting information carrying the risk of causing disturbances, disturbing order and safety, or terrorism;\n(b) Attacks on information systems important to national security;\n(c) Large-scale, high-intensity attacks on multiple information systems;\n(d) Cyberattacks intended to destroy important projects and important targets of national security;\n(dd) Cyberattacks that seriously infringe national sovereignty, interests and security and seriously damage social order and safety or the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·52·\n2. Responsibility for preventing dangerous cybersecurity situations is provided for as follows:\n(a) The specialised cybersecurity protection force shall coordinate with the person in charge of an information system important to national security in developing professional and technical solutions to prevent, detect and handle dangerous cybersecurity situations;\n(b) Telecommunications, Internet and information technology enterprises and providers of telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services in cyberspace, as well as the agencies, organisations and individuals concerned, are responsible for coordinating with the specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security in preventing, detecting and handling dangerous cybersecurity situations.\n3. Measures for handling dangerous cybersecurity situations comprise:\n(a) Immediately carrying out cybersecurity prevention and emergency response plans to stop, eliminate or mitigate the damage caused by the dangerous cybersecurity situation;\n(b) Notifying the agencies, organisations and individuals concerned;\n(c) Collecting relevant information and continuously tracking and supervising the dangerous cybersecurity situation;\n(d) Analysing and assessing information and predicting the likelihood, scope and extent of the damage that may be caused by the dangerous cybersecurity situation;\n(dd) Ceasing to provide online information in a specific area or disconnecting international network connection ports;\n(e) Deploying forces and means to stop and eliminate the dangerous cybersecurity situation;\n(g) Other measures provided for by the Law on National Security.\n4. The handling of dangerous cybersecurity situations is provided for as follows:\n(a) Upon detecting a dangerous cybersecurity situation, agencies, organisations and individuals shall promptly notify the specialised cybersecurity protection force and shall immediately adopt the measures provided for in items (a) and (b) of paragraph 3 of this Article.\n(b) The Prime Minister shall consider and decide, or authorise the Minister of Public Security to consider, decide and handle, dangerous cybersecurity situations nationwide, in each locality or in respect of specific targets.\nThe Prime Minister shall consider and decide, or authorise the Minister of National Defence to consider, decide and handle, dangerous cybersecurity situations in military information systems and in the cipher information systems under the Government Cipher Department.\n(c) The specialised cybersecurity protection force shall take the lead, in coordination with the relevant agencies, organisations and individuals, in adopting the measures provided for in paragraph 3 of this Article to handle dangerous cybersecurity situations.\n(d) The agencies, organisations and individuals concerned are responsible for coordinating with the specialised cybersecurity protection force in adopting measures to stop and handle dangerous cybersecurity situations."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 21"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 22,
        "text": {
          "en": "Cybersecurity protection combat\n1. Cybersecurity protection combat means the organised activities carried out by the specialised cybersecurity protection force in cyberspace to safeguard national security and social order and safety.\n2. The contents of cybersecurity protection combat comprise:\n(a) Grasping the situation relating to national security protection activities;\n·53· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam\n(b) Preventing and combating attacks on information systems important to national security and protecting their stable operation;\n(c) Eliminating and restricting activities that use cyberspace to harm national security or seriously harm social order and safety;\n(d) Taking the initiative to attack and deactivate targets in cyberspace in order to protect national security and ensure social order and safety.\n3. The Ministry of Public Security shall take the lead, in coordination with the relevant ministries, in conducting cybersecurity protection combat.\nChapter IV CYBERSECURITY PROTECTION ACTIVITIES"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 22"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 23,
        "text": {
          "en": "Cybersecurity protection activities in central and local State agencies and political organisations\n1. The contents of cybersecurity protection activities comprise:\n(a) Establishing and perfecting regulations on the use of internal computer networks and computer networks connected to the Internet, plans for ensuring the cybersecurity of information systems, and plans for responding to and resolving cybersecurity incidents;\n(b) Adopting and carrying out plans, measures and technologies to protect the cybersecurity of information systems within the scope of management and of the information and documents stored, compiled and transmitted in information systems;\n(c) Organising training on cybersecurity knowledge for cadres, public employees, public non-business unit staff and workers, and improving the cybersecurity protection capacity of the specialised cybersecurity protection force;\n(d) Protecting the cybersecurity of public service provision, the provision, exchange and collection of information on agencies, organisations and individuals, internal information sharing and information sharing with other agencies, and other activities provided for by the Government;\n(dd) Investing in and building physical infrastructure meeting the conditions for carrying out cybersecurity protection activities for information systems;\n(e) Inspecting the cybersecurity of information systems, preventing and combating violations of the law on cybersecurity, and responding to and eliminating cybersecurity incidents.\n2. Heads of agencies and organisations are responsible for carrying out cybersecurity protection activities within the scope of their management."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 23"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 24,
        "text": {
          "en": "Cybersecurity inspection of information systems of agencies and organisations not included in the list of information systems important to national security\n1. Cybersecurity inspection of information systems of agencies and organisations not included in the list of information systems important to national security shall be carried out in the following cases:\n(a) There are acts in violation of the law on cybersecurity, such as infringing national security or seriously harming social order and safety;\n(b) At the proposal of the person in charge of the information system.\n2. The objects of cybersecurity inspection comprise:\n(a) The system of hardware, software and digital devices used in the information system;\n(b) The information stored, processed and transmitted in the information system;\n(c) The measures for protecting State secrets and for preventing and combating the disclosure and loss of State secrets through technical means.\n3. When detecting acts in violation of the law on cybersecurity in the information system under his or her management, the person in charge of the information system is responsible for notifying the specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security.\n4. The specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security shall carry out cybersecurity inspection of the information systems of the agencies and organisations provided for in paragraph 1 of this Article.\n5. The specialised cybersecurity protection force shall notify the person in charge of the information system in writing at least 12 hours before carrying out the inspection.\nThe specialised cybersecurity protection force shall notify the results of the inspection within 30 days from the date of completion of the inspection; where confidentiality vulnerabilities or gaps are found, it shall make requests to the person in charge of the information system, or shall provide guidance or participate in remedying them at the proposal of the person in charge of the information system.\n6. The results of cybersecurity inspection shall be kept confidential in accordance with law.\n7. The procedures and formalities for cybersecurity inspection provided for in this Article shall be provided for by the Government."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 24"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 25,
        "text": {
          "en": "Cybersecurity protection of the national cyberspace infrastructure and international network interfaces\n1. The cybersecurity protection of the national cyberspace infrastructure and international network interfaces shall ensure a close combination of the requirements of cybersecurity protection with the requirements of socio-economic development; the establishment of international interfaces within the territory of Vietnam is encouraged; organisations and individuals are encouraged to participate in investment in the construction of the national cyberspace infrastructure.\n2. Agencies, organisations and individuals that manage and develop the national cyberspace infrastructure and international network interfaces have the following responsibilities:\n(a) To protect the cybersecurity of what they manage, and to accept the management, supervision and inspection of, and to comply with the requirements of, the competent State agencies in respect of cybersecurity protection.\n(b) To create conditions and adopt the necessary professional measures for the competent State agencies to carry out cybersecurity protection tasks, as recommended."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 25"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 26,
        "text": {
          "en": "Ensuring information security in cyberspace\n1. The websites, electronic portals and official social network pages of agencies, organisations and individuals may not provide, publish or disseminate information containing the contents provided for in paragraphs 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 of Article 16 of this Law or other information containing content infringing national security.\n2. Domestic and foreign enterprises providing telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services in cyberspace in Vietnam have the following responsibilities:\n(a) To verify information when users register digital accounts; to keep user information and accounts confidential; and to provide user information to the specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security upon written request, in order to serve the investigation and handling of violations of the law on cybersecurity.\n(b) Within 24 hours from the request of the specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security or the competent agency of the Ministry of Information and Communications, to stop the sharing of and delete service information provided for in paragraphs 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 of Article 16 of this Law or information in information systems directly managed by agencies and organisations, and to retain system logs in order to serve the investigation and handling of violations of the law on cybersecurity within the time limit provided for by the Government.\n(c) At the request of the specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security or the competent agency of the Ministry of Information and Communications, not to provide, or to stop providing, telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services to organisations and individuals that publish in ·55· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam cyberspace information containing the contents provided for in paragraphs 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 of Article 16 of this Law.\n3. Domestic and foreign enterprises providing telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services in cyberspace in Vietnam shall, within the time limit provided for by the Government, store in Vietnam the data on personal information, data on relationships between users and data created by Vietnamese users that they collect, exploit, analyse and process.\nForeign enterprises provided for in this Article must establish branches or representative offices in Vietnam.\n4. The Government shall provide specifically for paragraph 3 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 26"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 27,
        "text": {
          "en": "Research and development of cybersecurity\n1. The contents of research on and development of cybersecurity comprise:\n(a) Building systems of software and equipment for cybersecurity protection;\n(b) Methods of verifying whether cybersecurity protection software and equipment meet standards, and methods of limiting confidentiality vulnerabilities, gaps and malicious software;\n(c) Methods of checking whether the hardware and software supplied perform their functions correctly;\n(d) Methods of protecting State secrets, work secrets, business secrets, personal privacy and the privacy of family and private life, and the capacity to keep information transmission in cyberspace confidential;\n(dd) Determining the origin of information transmitted in cyberspace;\n(e) Resolving threats to cybersecurity;\n(g) Building cyber ranges and cybersecurity testing environments;\n(h) Technical measures to raise cybersecurity awareness and skills;\n(i) Cybersecurity forecasting;\n(k) Research on the practice and development of cybersecurity theory.\n2. The agencies, organisations and individuals concerned have the right to research and develop cybersecurity."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 27"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 28,
        "text": {
          "en": "Improving self-reliance in cybersecurity\n1. The State shall encourage and create conditions for improving the self-reliance in cybersecurity of agencies, organisations and individuals and for improving the capacity to produce, inspect, assess and appraise digital devices, network services and network applications.\n2. The Government shall adopt the following measures to improve the self-reliance in cybersecurity of agencies, organisations and individuals:\n(a) Promoting the transfer, research, mastery and development of technologies, products, services and applications for cybersecurity protection;\n(b) Promoting the application of new and advanced technologies relating to cybersecurity;\n(c) Organising the training, upgrading and use of cybersecurity personnel;\n(d) Strengthening the business environment and improving competitive conditions in order to help enterprises research and produce products, services and applications for cybersecurity protection.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·56·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 28"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 29,
        "text": {
          "en": "Protection of minors in cyberspace\n1. Minors participating in activities in cyberspace have the right to be protected, the right to access information, to participate in social and recreational activities, to have their private life kept confidential, and other rights.\n2. Persons in charge of information systems and providers of telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services in cyberspace are responsible for supervising the information content of information systems and of enterprise business in order not to harm minors or infringe minors and their rights; they are prohibited from sharing and must delete information containing content that harms minors or infringes minors and their rights, and shall promptly notify and coordinate with the specialised cybersecurity protection force of the Ministry of Public Security in handling it.\n3. Agencies, organisations and individuals participating in activities in cyberspace are responsible, in accordance with this Law and the law on minors, for coordinating with the competent agencies in safeguarding the rights of minors in cyberspace and for prohibiting information containing content harmful to minors.\n4. Agencies, organisations, parents, teachers, guardians and other persons concerned are responsible, in accordance with the law on minors, for safeguarding the rights of minors and protecting minors participating in activities in cyberspace.\n5. The specialised cybersecurity protection force and the functional agencies are responsible for adopting measures to prevent, detect, stop and strictly handle acts that use cyberspace to harm minors or infringe minors and their rights.\nChapter V ASSURANCE OF CYBERSECURITY PROTECTION ACTIVITIES"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 29"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 30,
        "text": {
          "en": "Specialised cybersecurity protection force\n1. The specialised cybersecurity protection force shall be deployed by the Ministry of Public Security and the Ministry of National Defence.\n2. The specialised cybersecurity protection force shall be deployed in the ministries, provincial-level People's Committees, agencies and organisations that directly manage information systems important to national security.\n3. Organisations and individuals may be mobilised to participate in cybersecurity protection."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 30"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 31,
        "text": {
          "en": "Personnel assurance for cybersecurity protection\n1. Vietnamese citizens with knowledge of cybersecurity, network information security and information technology constitute the fundamental and principal human resources for cybersecurity protection.\n2. The State shall have programmes and plans for building and developing cybersecurity protection human resources.\n3. In dangerous situations such as cybersecurity incidents, cyberterrorism, cyberattacks, cybersecurity incidents or threats to cybersecurity, the competent State agencies have the power to decide on the mobilisation of cybersecurity protection personnel.\nThe competence, responsibilities, procedures and formalities for the mobilisation of cybersecurity protection personnel shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of the Law on National Security, the Law on National Defence, the Law on the People's Public Security and other relevant laws."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 31"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 32,
        "text": {
          "en": "Recruitment, training and development of the specialised cybersecurity protection force\n1. Vietnamese citizens who meet the standards of ethical qualities, health and level of knowledge of cybersecurity, network information security and information technology and who so wish may be recruited into the specialised cybersecurity protection force.\n·57· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam\n2. Priority shall be given to training and developing a high-quality specialised cybersecurity protection force.\n3. Priority shall be given to developing cybersecurity training institutions meeting international standards, and joint ventures and cooperation in cybersecurity between the public and private sectors and between domestic and foreign parties shall be encouraged."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 32"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 33,
        "text": {
          "en": "Education and training in cybersecurity knowledge and professional skills\n1. The content of education and training in cybersecurity knowledge shall, in accordance with the law on national defence and security education, be incorporated into the national defence and security education curricula of schools and into the national defence and security knowledge training programmes.\n2. The Ministry of Public Security shall take the lead, in coordination with the relevant ministries, in organising professional cybersecurity training for the specialised cybersecurity protection force and for public employees, public non-business unit staff and workers participating in cybersecurity protection.\nThe Ministry of National Defence and the Government Cipher Department shall organise professional cybersecurity training for the persons under their management."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 33"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 34,
        "text": {
          "en": "Popularisation of cybersecurity knowledge\n1. The State shall have a policy of popularising cybersecurity knowledge throughout the country and encouraging State agencies, private organisations and individuals to coordinate in carrying out programmes to educate and raise awareness of cybersecurity.\n2. Ministries, agencies and organisations are responsible for formulating and carrying out activities to popularise cybersecurity knowledge for their cadres, public employees, public non-business unit staff and workers.\n3. Provincial-level People's Committees are responsible for formulating and carrying out activities to popularise cybersecurity knowledge and raise cybersecurity awareness for local agencies, organisations and individuals."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 34"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 35,
        "text": {
          "en": "Funding for cybersecurity protection\n1. Funding for the cybersecurity protection of State agencies and political organisations shall be borne by the State budget and included in the annual State budget plan. The management and use of State budget funding shall comply with the law on the State budget.\n2. Funding for the cybersecurity protection of the information systems of agencies and organisations other than those provided for in paragraph 1 of this Article shall be borne by those agencies and organisations themselves.\nChapter VI RESPONSIBILITIES OF AGENCIES, ORGANISATIONS AND INDIVIDUALS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 35"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 36,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of the Ministry of Public Security The Ministry of Public Security shall be accountable to the Government for the exercise of State management of cybersecurity and, apart from matters falling within the responsibility of the Ministry of National Defence and the Government Cipher Department, shall have the following tasks and powers:\n1. To issue, or submit to the competent State agency for issuance, legal provisions on cybersecurity and to guide their implementation.\n2. To formulate and propose strategies, guidelines, policies, plans and programmes for cybersecurity protection.\n3. To prevent and combat activities that use cyberspace to infringe national sovereignty, interests and security or social order and safety, and to prevent and combat cybercrime.\n4. To ensure information security in cyberspace; to establish a mechanism for authenticating the registration information of digital accounts; to warn of cybersecurity threats and to share cybersecurity information.\n5. Where the content of State management involves the scope of management of several ministries, to consult and propose to the Government and the Prime Minister for consideration and decision the assignment and coordination of measures to protect cybersecurity and to prevent and handle acts infringing cybersecurity.\n6. To organise drills on preventing and combating cyberattacks and drills on responding to and resolving cybersecurity incidents in information systems important to national security.\n7. To inspect and supervise, to settle complaints and denunciations and to handle violations of the law on cybersecurity."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 36"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 37,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of the Ministry of National Defence The Ministry of National Defence shall be accountable to the Government for the exercise of State management of cybersecurity within the scope of its management and shall have the following tasks and powers:\n1. To issue, or submit to the competent State agency for issuance, legal provisions on cybersecurity and to guide their implementation within the scope of its management;\n2. To formulate and propose strategies, lines, policies, plans and programmes for cybersecurity protection within the scope of its management;\n3. To prevent and combat activities that use cyberspace to infringe national security within the scope of its management;\n4. To coordinate with the Ministry of Public Security in organising drills on preventing and combating cyberattacks and on responding to and resolving cybersecurity incidents in information systems important to national security, and in carrying out cybersecurity protection work;\n5. To inspect and supervise, to settle complaints and denunciations and to handle violations of the law on cybersecurity within the scope of its management."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 37"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 38,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of the Ministry of Information and Communications\n1. To coordinate with the Ministry of Public Security and the Ministry of National Defence in protecting cybersecurity.\n2. To coordinate with the relevant agencies in organising dissemination to refute information against the Socialist Republic of Vietnam containing the contents provided for in paragraph 1 of Article 16 of this Law.\n3. To request providers of telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services in cyberspace and persons in charge of information systems to delete information containing content in violation of the law on cybersecurity in the services and information systems directly managed by enterprises, agencies and organisations."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 38"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 39,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of the Government Cipher Department\n1. To consult and propose to the Minister of National Defence for issuance, or submit to the competent issuing agency, legal provisions, projects and plans on cryptography for cybersecurity protection, and to organise their implementation within the scope of its management.\n2. To protect the cybersecurity of the cipher information systems under the Government Cipher Department and of the cipher products whose supply is provided for by this Law.\n3. To exercise unified management of research in cipher science and technology and of the production, use and supply of cipher products in order to protect State secret information stored and exchanged in cyberspace."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 39"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 40,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of ministries and provincial-level People's Committees Ministries and provincial-level People's Committees shall, within the scope of their competence, be responsible for carrying out cybersecurity protection work in respect of the information and information systems under their management, and shall coordinate with the Ministry of Public Security in the exercise of State management of cybersecurity in the ministries and localities.\n·59· The Law on Cybersecurity of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 40"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 41,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of providers of services in cyberspace\n1. Providers of services in cyberspace in Vietnam have the following responsibilities:\n(a) To warn of the possibility of cybersecurity failure arising in the course of use of the services they provide in cyberspace, and to guide the implementation of preventive measures.\n(b) To formulate contingency plans and solutions for cybersecurity incidents and to deal immediately with confidentiality vulnerabilities and gaps, malicious code, cyberattacks, network intrusions and other security risks; when a cybersecurity incident occurs, to carry out immediately the corresponding contingency plans and response measures and at the same time to notify the specialised cybersecurity protection force in accordance with this Law.\n(c) To adopt technical solutions and other necessary measures to ensure the security of the information collection process and to prevent the risk of data exposure, leakage, damage or loss; where an incident of exposure, leakage, damage or loss of user information occurs or exists, to propose immediately response measures and at the same time to notify users and report to the specialised cybersecurity protection force in accordance with this Law.\n(d) To coordinate with, and create conditions for, the specialised cybersecurity protection force in carrying out cybersecurity protection.\n2. Providers of telecommunications network services, Internet services and value-added services in cyberspace in Vietnam are responsible for complying with paragraph 1 of this Article and paragraphs 1, 2 and 3 of Article 26 of this Law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 41"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 42,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of agencies, organisations and individuals using cyberspace\n1. To comply with the provisions of the law on cybersecurity.\n2. To provide promptly to the competent agencies and the specialised cybersecurity protection force information relating to cybersecurity protection, cybersecurity threats and acts infringing cybersecurity.\n3. To comply with the requirements and instructions of the competent agencies on cybersecurity protection, and to create conditions for and assist agencies, organisations and persons responsible in carrying out cybersecurity protection measures.\nChapter VII IMPLEMENTING PROVISIONS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 42"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 43,
        "text": {
          "en": "Effect\n1. This Law shall take effect on 1 January 2019.\n2. Where an information system in operation and use is included in the list of information systems important to national security, the person in charge of the information system is responsible, within 12 months from the date on which this Law takes effect, for ensuring that it satisfies cybersecurity conditions, and the specialised cybersecurity protection force shall assess the cybersecurity conditions in accordance with Article 12 of this Law; where an extension is necessary, the Prime Minister shall decide, but for not more than 12 months.\n3. Where an information system in operation and use is added to the list of information systems important to national security, the person in charge of the information system is responsible, within 12 months from the date of addition, for ensuring that it satisfies cybersecurity conditions, and the specialised cybersecurity protection force shall assess the cybersecurity conditions in accordance with Article 12 of this Law; where an extension is necessary, the Prime Minister shall decide, but for not more than 12 months.\nThis Law was adopted on 12 June 2018 at the Fifth Session of the Fourteenth National Assembly of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 43"
        }
      }
    ]
  };

  TR["VN-H070"] = {
    "lawId": "VN-H070",
    "sourceLang": "zh",
    "targetLang": "en",
    "engine": "llm",
    "model": "deepseek-v4.1-flash",
    "promptVersion": "calr-vi2en-v1",
    "translatedAt": "2026-10-28",
    "reviewed": "machine",
    "completenessNote": "逐条对译，共 77 条；条号与原文一一对应，未合并、未省略。",
    "articles": [
      {
        "no": 1,
        "text": {
          "en": "·3· Pursuant to the Constitution of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam, the National Assembly promulgates the Law on Civil Status of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam.\nChapter I GENERAL PROVISIONS\nScope of application\n1. This Law regulates civil status work: the rights, obligations, principles, competence and procedures for civil status registration, the civil status database and the State management of civil status.\n2. The competence and procedures for dealing with matters of nationality and of adoption shall, unless otherwise provided by this Law, comply with the Law on Vietnamese Nationality and the Law on Adoption."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 1"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 2,
        "text": {
          "en": "Civil status and civil status registration\n1. Civil status means the aggregate of the information provided for in this Law"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 2"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 3,
        "text": {
          "en": "that serves to determine a person's identity information from birth to death.\n2. Civil status registration means the confirmation or recording of a person's civil status by a State authority, thereby laying a legal foundation for the State to protect the rights and interests of the individual and to carry out population management.\nArticle 3  Contents of civil status registration\n1. The following civil status information shall be registered in the civil status book:\n(a) birth;\n(b) marital status;\n(c) guardianship;\n(d) adoption;\n(đ) change or correction of civil status, re-determination of ethnicity, and supplementation of civil status information;\n(e) death.\n2. Changes in a person's civil status shall be registered in the civil status book in accordance with the regulations of the competent authority:\n(a) change of nationality;\n(b) determination of a parent-child relationship;\n* Translator: Du Xuemiao, Assistant Lecturer, School of Asian Studies, Beijing Foreign Studies University.\n* Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·4· (c) re-determination of sex;\n(d) adoption and termination of adoption;\n(đ) divorce, annulment of an unlawful marriage, and publication of a marriage;\n(e) recognition of a guardian;\n(g) declaration of a person as missing or dead, or restriction of civil act capacity.\n3. Information on the birth, marriage, divorce, annulment of marriage, guardianship, parent-child relationship, adoption, change of civil status and death of Vietnamese citizens registered with a foreign authority shall be recorded in the civil status book.\n4. Other civil status information shall be confirmed or registered in the civil status book in accordance with law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 3"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 4,
        "text": {
          "en": "Interpretation of terms Certain terms in this Law are interpreted as follows:\n1. Civil status registration agencies are the People's Committees of communes, wards and towns (hereinafter referred to as \"commune-level People's Committees\"), the People's Committees of districts, sub-districts, towns and cities under provinces and equivalent administrative units (hereinafter referred to as \"district-level People's Committees\"), and Vietnam's diplomatic missions and consular missions abroad (hereinafter referred to as \"representative missions\").\n2. The civil status database means the collection of a person's civil status information registered and retained in the civil status book and in the electronic civil status database.\n3. The civil status book means the paper book prepared and kept at the civil status registration agency and used to confirm or record the civil status information provided for in Article 3 of this Law.\n4. The electronic civil status database means the sectoral database formed through the computerisation of civil status registration work.\n5. The civil status database management agencies comprise the civil status registration agencies empowered by law, the Ministry of Justice, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and other agencies.\n6. A birth certificate means the document issued by a State authority to an individual upon birth registration, containing the basic personal information provided for in clause 1 of Article 14 of this Law.\n7. A marriage certificate means the document issued by a State authority to a man and a woman upon marriage registration, containing the basic information provided for in clause 2 of Article 17 of this Law.\n8. Native place means the place of origin of the person's father or mother, as agreed by the parents or in accordance with custom, recorded in the birth certificate upon birth registration.\n9. A civil status extract means the document issued by a State authority to certify a person's civil status information registered at a civil status registration agency, issued after the civil status information has been registered; its copies comprise copies printed from the civil status database and copies certified against the original.\n10. Change of civil status means the change, made by a State authority at the request of an individual on legitimate grounds in accordance with the civil law, of the individual's or the parents' civil status information in the birth information already registered.\n11. Re-determination of ethnicity means the registration by a State authority, in accordance with the civil law, of an individual's ethnicity as re-determined.\n·5·\n12. Correction of civil status means the rectification of civil status information by a State authority where the civil status registration contains an error.\n13. Supplementation of civil status means the updating by a State authority of civil status information omitted at the time of the individual's registration."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 4"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 5,
        "text": {
          "en": "Principles of civil status registration\n1. Respect for and protection of individual rights.\n2. All of a person's civil status information must be registered fully, promptly, truthfully, objectively and accurately; where the conditions for civil status registration are not met in accordance with law, the head of the civil status registration agency may refuse to register on the basis of documentary evidence and shall state the reasons.\n3. Civil status matters for which this Law does not prescribe a processing time limit may be dealt with on the same day. If a matter cannot be settled promptly within 15 hours of receipt of the dossier, a reply shall be given on the next working day.\n4. Each item of civil status information may be registered only at the competent civil status registration agency provided for in this Law.\nAn individual may register civil status at the civil status registration agency of the place of permanent residence, the place of temporary residence or the place where he or she is currently present. Where an individual registers elsewhere than at the place of permanent residence, the district-level People's Committee, commune-level People's Committee or local representative mission that has registered the individual's civil status shall report the civil status registration to the commune-level People's Committee of the individual's place of permanent residence.\n5. All civil status information recorded in the civil status book must be promptly and fully updated in the electronic civil status database.\n6. Personal information in the civil status database on birth, marriage, divorce, death, change, correction and supplementation of civil status, sex and ethnicity constitutes primary information in the national population database.\n7. Ensuring that the civil status registration process is open and transparent."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 5"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 6,
        "text": {
          "en": "Rights and obligations of individuals regarding civil status registration\n1. Vietnamese citizens and stateless persons permanently residing in Vietnam have the right and the obligation to register their civil status.\nExcept where otherwise provided by an international treaty to which Vietnam is a party, this provision also applies to foreign citizens permanently residing in Vietnam.\n2. When registering a marriage or determining an adoption relationship, the parties must appear in person at the civil status registration agency.\nFor other civil status registration matters or for the issue of copies of civil status extracts, the person concerned may act in person or authorise another person to act.\nThe Minister of Justice shall make specific provision on authorising another person to act.\n3. Minors and persons who have reached full age but have lost civil act capacity shall register their civil status or apply for copies of civil status extracts through their representatives in accordance with law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 6"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 7,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for civil status registration\n1. Commune-level People's Committees shall carry out civil status registration in the following cases:\n(a) registering, for Vietnamese citizens residing in the country, the matters specified in points (a), (b), (c), (d) and (e) of clause 1 of Article 3 of this Law;\n(b) carrying out the change and correction of civil status for persons under 14 years of age and supplementing civil status information for Vietnamese citizens residing in the country;\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·6· (c) carrying out civil status registration in accordance with clauses 2 and 4 of Article 3 of this Law;\n(d) registering the birth of a child born in Vietnam whose father or mother is a Vietnamese citizen permanently residing in a border area and the other is a citizen of a neighbouring country permanently residing in the border area; registering the marriage and the determination of an adoption relationship between a Vietnamese citizen permanently residing in a border area and a citizen of a neighbouring country permanently residing in the border area; and registering the death of a foreign citizen long resident in Vietnam.\n2. Apart from the cases provided for in point (d) of clause 1 of this Article, district-level People's Committees shall carry out civil status registration in the following cases:\n(a) civil status registration under clause 1 of Article 3 of this Law involving a foreign element;\n(b) change and correction of civil status and determination of ethnicity for Vietnamese citizens aged 14 years or over residing in the country;\n(c) civil status matters in accordance with clause 3 of Article 3 of this Law.\n3. Representative missions shall carry out civil status matters for Vietnamese citizens residing abroad in accordance with Article 3 of this Law.\n4. The Government shall provide for the procedures for registering birth, marriage, determination of an adoption relationship and death under point (d) of clause 1 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 7"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 8,
        "text": {
          "en": "Guarantee of the rights and obligations regarding civil status registration\n1. The State has policies and measures to create conditions for individuals to exercise their rights and to fulfil their obligations regarding civil status registration.\n2. The State shall provide support for finance, material facilities and human resources and shall invest in information technology development for civil status registration and management."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 8"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 9,
        "text": {
          "en": "Applications for civil status registration and the manner of receiving them\n1. When registering civil status or applying for copies of civil status extracts, an individual shall submit the dossier in person to the civil status registration agency or send it to that agency by post or through the online civil status registration system.\n2. When carrying out civil status registration procedures or printing copies of civil status extracts from the civil status database, an individual shall present his or her identity card to the civil status registration agency, except where the dossier is sent by post, in which case a copy of the identity card must be enclosed.\n3. For civil status matters for which a processing time limit is prescribed, the person receiving the dossier must issue a receipt; where the dossier is incomplete or invalid, he or she must guide the applicant, in accordance with the documentary regulations, on supplementing and completing the civil status application, and the written guidance must be complete in content and state what is to be supplemented.\nWhere the dossier submitted by the individual does not meet the requirements of the civil status registration agency, the person receiving it shall guide the individual to submit the dossier to the competent authority."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 9"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 10,
        "text": {
          "en": "Consular legalisation of foreign certificates Unless consular legalisation is exempted because Vietnam is a party to an international treaty, certificates issued, notarised or certified by a foreign authority for use in civil status registration in Vietnam must be consularly legalised in accordance with law.\n·7·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 10"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 11,
        "text": {
          "en": "Civil status registration fees\n1. Civil status registration fees shall be waived in the following cases:\n(a) civil status registration for members of families that have rendered meritorious service to the revolution, poor households and persons with disabilities;\n(b) registration, within the prescribed time limit, of birth, death, guardianship and marital status information for Vietnamese citizens residing in the country.\n2. An individual who requests the registration of civil status information other than that provided for in clause 1 of this Article, or who applies for copies of civil status extracts, shall pay a fee.\nThe Ministry of Finance shall make specific provision on the competence to collect fees, the level of fees collected and paid, and the management regime and use of the fees."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 11"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 12,
        "text": {
          "en": "Prohibited acts\n1. Individuals are strictly prohibited from committing the following acts:\n(a) providing information and materials inconsistent with the facts, making false certifications or using forged certificates or certificates belonging to other persons, in civil status registration;\n(b) coercing, threatening or obstructing another person in civil status registration;\n(c) unlawfully interfering in civil status registration work;\n(d) giving guarantees or certifications inconsistent with the facts in order to register civil status;\n(đ) falsifying or altering civil status certificates or information in the civil status database;\n(e) bribing, suborning or promising material or spiritual benefits in order to register civil status;\n(g) seeking profit in any form, taking advantage of civil status registration to enjoy State preferential policies, or evading the obligation to register civil status;\n(h) an individual has the right, in accordance with the Law on Marriage and Family, to register civil status on his or her own behalf or on behalf of a relative;\n(i) unlawfully logging into the civil status database, or stealing or destroying information in the civil status database.\n2. Civil status certificates issued in breach of points (a), (d), đ and (g) of clause 1 of this Article shall be invalid and must be withdrawn and destroyed.\n3. Individuals who commit the acts provided for in clause 1 of this Article shall, depending on the nature and seriousness of the violation, be subject to administrative sanctions or to examination of criminal liability in accordance with law.\nOfficials and public employees who breach clause 1 of this Article shall, in addition to the above sanctions, be subject to disciplinary measures in accordance with the law on officials and public employees.\nChapter II CIVIL STATUS REGISTRATION BY COMMUNE-LEVEL PEOPLE'S COMMITTEES Section 1 BIRTH REGISTRATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 12"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 13,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for birth registration Birth registration shall be carried out at the commune-level People's Committee of the place of residence of the father or of the mother.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·8·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 13"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 14,
        "text": {
          "en": "Contents of birth registration\n1. The contents of birth registration comprise:\n(a) personal information of the person whose birth is registered: family name, middle name, given name, sex, date of birth, place of birth, native place, ethnicity and nationality;\n(b) information on the parents of the person whose birth is registered: family name, middle name, given name, sex, year of birth, ethnicity, nationality and place of residence;\n(c) the identity card number of the person whose birth is registered.\n2. The nationality, ethnicity and family name of the person whose birth is registered shall be determined in accordance with the Law on Vietnamese Nationality and the Civil Code.\n3. The contents of birth registration provided for in clause 1 of this Article constitute the basic civil status information of the individual and shall be recorded in the civil status book, the birth certificate, the electronic civil status database and the national population database; the personal documents and certificates relating to the birth information must be consistent with the birth registration information of the person concerned.\nThe Government shall provide for the issue of the identity card number of the person whose birth is registered."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 14"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 15,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibility for birth registration\n1. Within 60 days from the birth of a child, the father or the mother has the responsibility to register the birth; where the father or the mother is unable to do so, the grandfather, grandmother or other relative, individual or organisation raising the child has the responsibility to register the birth.\n2. Judicial-civil status officers shall, within the prescribed time limit, check and urge birth registration in their localities and, where necessary, may carry out mobile birth registration."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 15"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 16,
        "text": {
          "en": "Birth registration procedure\n1. A person registering a birth shall submit to the civil status registration agency the form set by the prescribed template and the birth certificate; where there is no birth certificate, a document made by the person who witnessed the birth, and where there is no witness, a written guarantee of the birth; the birth registration of an abandoned child must be accompanied by the certificate of abandonment issued by the competent authority; the birth registration of a child born by surrogacy must be accompanied by the document on the surrogacy issued in accordance with law.\n2. After receiving the certificates in full in accordance with clause 1 of this Article, if the birth information is complete and in accordance with regulations, the judicial-civil status officer shall register the birth information in the civil status book, the electronic civil status database and the national population database in accordance with clause 1 of Article 14 of this Law in order to obtain an identity card number.\nThe judicial-civil status officer and the person registering the birth shall both sign the civil status book, and the Chairman of the commune-level People's Committee shall issue the birth certificate to the person whose birth is registered.\n3. The Government shall make specific provision on the registration of the births of abandoned children, of children whose parents have not been determined and of children born by surrogacy, and on the determination of the native place of abandoned children and of children whose parents have not been determined.\n·9· Section 2 MARRIAGE REGISTRATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 16"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 17,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for marriage registration and contents of the marriage certificate\n1. Marriage registration shall be carried out at the commune-level People's Committee of the place of residence of either the man or the woman.\n2. The marriage certificate must contain the following information:\n(a) the family name, middle name, given name, date of birth, ethnicity, nationality, place of residence and identity card information of the man and of the woman;\n(b) the date of registration of the marriage;\n(c) the signatures or fingerprints of the man and of the woman and the certification of the civil status registration agency."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 17"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 18,
        "text": {
          "en": "Marriage registration procedure\n1. The man and the woman shall submit the marriage registration form set by the prescribed template to the civil status registration agency, and both must be present at the marriage registration.\n2. After receiving the certificates in full in accordance with clause 1 of this Article, if the conditions for marriage provided for in the Law on Marriage and Family are satisfied, the judicial-civil status officer shall record the marriage information in the civil status book and shall sign it together with the man and the woman. The man and the woman shall both sign the marriage certificate, and the judicial-civil status officer shall notify the Chairman of the commune-level People's Committee to issue the marriage certificate to the man and the woman.\nWhere it is necessary to verify the conditions for marriage of the man and the woman, the processing time limit shall not exceed 5 working days.\nSection 3 GUARDIANSHIP REGISTRATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 18"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 19,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for guardianship registration and termination of guardianship The commune-level People's Committee of the place of residence of the ward or of the guardian shall carry out guardianship registration.\nThe commune-level People's Committee that carried out the guardianship registration shall carry out the termination of guardianship."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 19"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 20,
        "text": {
          "en": "Procedure for registration of guardianship by designation\n1. A person applying for guardianship registration shall submit to the civil status registration agency the guardianship registration form set by the prescribed template and the application for designation of a guardian, in accordance with the Civil Code.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, within 3 working days from the date of receipt of the certificates in full, if the conditions provided by law are satisfied, the judicial-civil status officer shall record it in the civil status book and sign it together with the person registering the guardianship, and shall report to the Chairman of the commune-level People's Committee for the issue of an extract."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 20"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 21,
        "text": {
          "en": "Registration of guardianship arising by operation of law\n1. A person applying for guardianship registration shall submit to the civil status registration agency the guardianship registration form set by the prescribed template and the form certifying the conditions for guardianship arising by operation of law. Where several persons simultaneously satisfy the conditions for being a guardian by operation of law, an agreement designating one guardian by operation of law must also be submitted.\n2. The procedure for registering a guardian by operation of law shall be carried out in accordance with clause 2 of Article 20 of this Law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 21"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 22,
        "text": {
          "en": "Registration of termination of guardianship\n1. A person applying for registration of the termination of guardianship shall submit to the civil status registration agency the guardianship registration form set by the prescribed template Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·10· and the certificate of termination of guardianship, in accordance with the Civil Code.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, within 2 working days from the date of receipt of the certificates in full, if the termination of guardianship satisfies the conditions provided by law, the judicial-civil status officer shall register it in the civil status book and sign it together with the person registering the termination of guardianship, and shall report to the Chairman of the commune-level People's Committee for the issue of an extract."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 22"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 23,
        "text": {
          "en": "Registration of change of guardianship Where a change of guardian is applied for in accordance with the Civil Code and another person satisfies the conditions for being a guardian, the parties shall first carry out the procedure for terminating the guardianship and shall then register the new guardian in accordance with the provisions of this Section.\nSection 4 ADOPTION REGISTRATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 23"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 24,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for registration of adoption\n1. A person applying for registration of an adoption shall submit to the civil status registration agency the form set by the prescribed template and the document evidencing the parent-child relationship; all parties, including the child, must be present at the registration.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, within 3 working days from the date of receipt of the certificates in full, if the adoption is lawful and undisputed, the judicial-civil status officer shall record it in the civil status book and sign it together with the person registering, and shall report to the Chairman of the commune-level People's Committee for the issue of an extract.\nWhere certificates must be produced, the time limit shall be extended by not more than 5 working days."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 24"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 25,
        "text": {
          "en": "(missing) Section 5 REGISTRATION OF CHANGE, CORRECTION AND SUPPLEMENTATION OF CIVIL STATUS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 25"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 26,
        "text": {
          "en": "Scope of change of civil status\n1. The family name, middle name and given name of an individual in the birth information already registered may be changed where there are grounds in accordance with the civil law.\n2. The information on the parents in the birth information already registered may be changed where the person is adopted in accordance with the law on adoption."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 26"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 27,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for registration of change, correction and supplementation of civil status The commune-level People's Committee that previously registered the civil status, or of the place of residence of the individual, is competent to change or correct the civil status of persons under 14 years of age and to supplement the civil status of Vietnamese citizens residing in the country."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 27"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 28,
        "text": {
          "en": "Procedure for registration of change and correction of civil status\n1. A person applying for registration of a change or correction of civil status shall submit to the civil status registration agency the form set by the prescribed template and the relevant certificates.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, within 3 working days from the date of receipt of the certificates in full, if the change or correction of civil status is well founded and complies with the civil law and the relevant laws, the judicial-civil status officer shall record it in the civil status book and sign it together with the applicant, and shall report to the Chairman of the commune-level People's Committee for the issue of an extract.\nWhere the change or correction of civil status relates to the birth certificate or the marriage certificate, the judicial-civil status officer shall record it in the birth certificate or the marriage certificate as well.\n·11·\n3. Where a change or correction of civil status is made elsewhere than at the place where the civil status was previously registered, the commune-level People's Committee must report, with the certificate and a copy of the civil status extract, to the People's Committee of the place of the previous civil status registration so that it may record the change in the civil status book.\nWhere the place of the previous civil status registration is a representative mission, the commune-level People's Committee must report, with the certificate and a copy of the civil status extract, to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs so that it may transmit the information to the representative mission for recording in the civil status book."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 28"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 29,
        "text": {
          "en": "Procedure for supplementation of civil status\n1. A person applying for supplementation of civil status shall submit to the civil status registration agency the form set by the prescribed template and the relevant certificates.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, after receiving the certificates in full, if the application for supplementation of civil status is well founded, the judicial-civil status officer shall record the supplemented content in the corresponding section of the civil status book and sign it together with the applicant, and shall report to the Chairman of the commune-level People's Committee for the issue of an extract.\nWhere the supplemented civil status information is to be recorded in the birth certificate or the marriage certificate, the judicial-civil status officer may record it in the corresponding place and affix a seal thereto.\nSection 6 RECORDING IN THE CIVIL STATUS BOOK OF CHANGES OF CIVIL STATUS UNDER JUDGMENTS AND DECISIONS OF COMPETENT AUTHORITIES"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 29"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 30,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibility for notification of changes of civil status\n1. In accordance with clause 2 of Article 3 of this Law, within 5 working days from the date on which the judgment or decision on the change of civil status takes legal effect, the People's Court or the State authority has the responsibility to notify, in the form of a document accompanied by an extract of the judgment or a copy of the decision, the People's Committee of the place of civil status registration of the individual so that it may record the change in the civil status book; where the place of civil status registration is a representative mission, the notification shall be sent to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs so that it may transmit it to the representative mission for recording in the civil status book.\n2. Notification of a change of nationality shall be carried out in accordance with the law on nationality."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 30"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 31,
        "text": {
          "en": "Procedure for recording changes of civil status in the civil status book under judgments and decisions of State authorities In accordance with Article 30 of this Law, upon receipt of the notification, the judicial-civil status officer shall register the changed content in the civil status book in accordance with the judgment or decision of the People's Court or of the State authority.\nSection 7 DEATH REGISTRATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 31"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 32,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for death registration Death registration shall be carried out at the commune-level People's Committee of the last place of residence of the deceased. Where the last place of residence of the deceased cannot be determined, the death registration shall be carried out at the commune-level People's Committee of the place where the person died or where the body was found."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 32"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 33,
        "text": {
          "en": "Time limit and responsibility for death registration\n1. Within 15 days from the date of death, the wife or husband, children, parents or other relatives of the deceased have the responsibility to register the death; where the deceased has no relatives, the representative of the agency, organisation or individual concerned shall register the death.\n2. Judicial-civil status officers shall regularly check death registration and urge the registration of deaths. Where the person responsible for registering a death cannot be determined, the judicial-civil status officer shall register the death.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·12·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 33"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 34,
        "text": {
          "en": "Death registration procedure\n1. The person responsible for death registration shall submit to the civil status registration agency the form set by the prescribed template and the notice of death or another certificate in place of the notice of death.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, after receiving the certificates in full, if the application for death registration is true, the judicial-civil status officer shall record the death registration information in the civil status book, sign it together with the applicant and report to the Chairman of the commune-level People's Committee for the issue of an extract.\nThe judicial-civil status officer shall lock the civil status information of the deceased in the electronic civil status database.\nChapter III CIVIL STATUS REGISTRATION BY DISTRICT-LEVEL PEOPLE'S COMMITTEES Section 1 BIRTH REGISTRATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 34"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 35,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for birth registration In the following cases, the district-level People's Committee of the place of residence of the father or the mother shall carry out the birth registration of the newborn:\n1. The newborn was born in Vietnam:\n(a) one parent is a Vietnamese citizen and the other is a foreign citizen or a stateless person;\n(b) one parent is a Vietnamese citizen residing in the country and the other is a Vietnamese citizen settled abroad;\n(c) the parents are Vietnamese citizens settled abroad;\n(d) the parents are foreign citizens or stateless persons.\n2. The newborn was born abroad and has not returned to Vietnam to have the birth registered at the place of residence:\n(a) both parents are Vietnamese citizens;\n(b) one parent is a Vietnamese citizen."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 35"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 36,
        "text": {
          "en": "Birth registration procedure\n1. In accordance with clause 1 of Article 16 of this Law, the person applying for birth registration shall submit the certificates to the civil status registration agency; where one or both of the parents are foreign citizens, an agreement on the choice of the child's nationality must also be submitted.\nWhere the parents choose a foreign nationality for the child, the agreement must be certified by the foreign State authority of the party who is a foreign citizen.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, after receiving the certificates in full, if the birth registration information is complete and in accordance with regulations, the civil status officer shall record the birth information in the civil status book in accordance with Article 14 of this Law; in accordance with point (c) of clause 1 of Article 14 of this Law, where the newborn has a foreign nationality, the birth information shall not be recorded in the civil status book.\nThe civil status officer and the person applying for birth registration shall both sign the civil status book, and the Justice Office shall report to the Chairman of the district-level People's Committee for the issue of a birth certificate.\n·13·\n3. The Government shall provide for the birth registration procedure referred to in clause 2 of Article 35 of this Law.\nSection 2 MARRIAGE REGISTRATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 36"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 37,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for marriage registration\n1. The district-level People's Committee of the place of residence of the Vietnamese citizen shall carry out the following marriage registrations: marriage between a Vietnamese citizen and a foreign citizen, marriage between a Vietnamese citizen residing in the country and a Vietnamese citizen settled abroad, marriage between Vietnamese citizens settled abroad, and marriage between a Vietnamese citizen who also holds a foreign nationality and a Vietnamese citizen or a foreign citizen.\n2. Where foreigners residing in Vietnam apply for marriage registration in Vietnam, the marriage registration shall be carried out by the district-level People's Committee of the place of residence of one of the parties."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 37"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 38,
        "text": {
          "en": "Marriage registration procedure\n1. The man and the woman shall submit to the civil status registration agency the form set by the prescribed template and a certificate issued by a competent medical institution in Vietnam or abroad confirming that neither of them suffers from a mental illness resulting in loss of awareness and of the capacity to act.\nForeigners and Vietnamese citizens settled abroad must also submit a certificate of marital status and a copy of their passport or a valid document in place of the passport.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, within 15 days from the date of receipt of the certificates in full, the civil status officer has the responsibility to verify.\nWhere the conditions for marriage provided by law are satisfied, the Justice Office shall report to the district-level People's Committee for implementation.\n3. At the marriage registration, the man and the woman must appear at the office of the People's Committee; the civil status officer shall ask for the opinions of the man and the woman and, if both consent to the marriage, shall record the marriage information in the civil status book and sign it together with the man and the woman. The man and the woman shall both sign the marriage certificate.\nThe Chairman of the district-level People's Committee shall issue the marriage certificate to the man and the woman.\n4. The Government has provided for the following: the supplementation of certificates in the marriage registration dossier; the questioning and verification of the purpose of marriage at the time of marriage registration; and the issue of certificates of marital status to Vietnamese citizens who have registered a marriage with a foreign authority in order to protect the rights and interests of the parties.\nSection 3 GUARDIANSHIP REGISTRATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 38"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 39,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for guardianship registration and termination of guardianship The district-level People's Committee of the place of residence of the ward or of the guardian shall carry out guardianship registration for Vietnamese citizens and foreigners residing together in Vietnam.\nThe district-level People's Committee that carried out the guardianship registration shall carry out the termination of guardianship."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 39"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 40,
        "text": {
          "en": "Procedure for registration of guardianship by designation\n1. A person applying for guardianship registration shall submit to the civil status registration agency the guardianship registration form set by the prescribed template and the application for designation of a guardian, in accordance with the Civil Code.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·14·\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, within 5 working days from the date of receipt of the certificates in full, the civil status officer shall carry out verification and, if the conditions provided by law are satisfied, shall record it in the civil status book and sign it together with the person registering the guardianship, and the Justice Office shall report to the Chairman of the district-level People's Committee to issue an extract to the applicant."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 40"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 41,
        "text": {
          "en": "Registration of guardianship arising by operation of law The procedure for registering guardianship arising by operation of law between Vietnamese citizens and foreigners residing together in Vietnam shall apply Article 21 of this Law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 41"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 42,
        "text": {
          "en": "Procedure for registration of termination and change of guardianship The procedure for registering the termination and change of guardianship between Vietnamese citizens and foreigners shall apply Articles 22 and 23 of this Law.\nSection 4 ADOPTION REGISTRATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 42"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 43,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for registration of adoption The district-level People's Committee of the place of residence of the adopted person shall carry out the following adoption registrations: adoption between a Vietnamese citizen and a foreign citizen, adoption between a Vietnamese citizen residing in the country and a Vietnamese citizen settled abroad, adoption between Vietnamese citizens settled abroad, adoption between a Vietnamese citizen who also holds a foreign nationality and a Vietnamese citizen or a foreign citizen, and adoption between foreigners one or both of whom permanently reside in Vietnam."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 43"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 44,
        "text": {
          "en": "Adoption registration procedure\n1. A person applying for registration of an adoption shall submit to the civil status registration agency the form set by the prescribed template and the documents, articles or other evidence capable of proving the parent-child relationship. The registration of an adoption between a Vietnamese citizen and a foreign citizen, or between foreigners, must be accompanied by a copy of the passport or a valid document in place of the passport capable of proving identity.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, within 15 days from the date of receipt of the certificates in full, the civil status officer shall carry out verification and shall post the adoption publicly at the office of the district-level People's Committee for 7 consecutive days, and shall at the same time send the documents to the commune-level People's Committee of the place of permanent residence of the adopted person, requesting that it post them publicly at the office of the commune-level People's Committee for 7 consecutive days.\n3. The Justice Office shall report to and request the Chairman of the district-level People's Committee to make provision on the registration of the adoption and, if the conditions are satisfied, the Chairman of the district-level People's Committee shall carry it out.\n4. At the registration of the adoption all parties must be present; the civil status officer shall record the adoption information in the civil status book and sign it together with the parties, and the Chairman of the district-level People's Committee shall issue an extract to the parties.\nSection 5 CHANGE, CORRECTION AND SUPPLEMENTATION OF CIVIL STATUS AND RE-DETERMINATION OF ETHNICITY"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 44"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 45,
        "text": {
          "en": "Scope of change of civil status The scope of change of civil status shall apply Article 26 of this Law.\n·15·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 45"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 46,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence to carry out the change, correction and supplementation of civil status and the re-determination of ethnicity\n1. For foreigners whose civil status has already been registered with a Vietnamese authority, the district-level People's Committee of the place of the previous civil status registration or of the place of residence of the person concerned is competent to carry out the change, correction and supplementation of civil status.\n2. The district-level People's Committee of the place where a Vietnamese person settled abroad previously registered civil status is competent to carry out the change, correction and supplementation of civil status and the re-determination of ethnicity.\n3. The district-level People's Committee of the place of residence or of the place of the previous civil status registration of the individual is competent to carry out the change and correction of civil status for Vietnamese citizens aged 14 years or over residing in the country, and is also competent to carry out the re-determination of ethnicity."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 46"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 47,
        "text": {
          "en": "Procedure for the change, correction and supplementation of civil status and the re-determination of ethnicity\n1. The procedure for the change and correction of civil status shall apply Article 28 of this Law.\nAn application for determination of ethnicity must be accompanied by the certificates prescribed by law, and the procedure shall be carried out in accordance with Article 28 of this Law.\n2. The procedure for supplementing civil status information shall apply Article 29 of this Law.\nSection 6 RECORDING IN THE CIVIL STATUS BOOK OF CIVIL STATUS MATTERS PERFORMED FOR VIETNAMESE CITIZENS BY FOREIGN AUTHORITIES"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 47"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 48,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence to record in the civil status book civil status matters performed for Vietnamese citizens by foreign authorities\n1. The district-level People's Committee of the place of residence of the Vietnamese citizen shall record in the civil status book the birth, marriage, guardianship, adoption and change of civil status performed by a foreign authority.\n2. The district-level People's Committee of the place of the previous marriage registration or of the place of annotation shall record in the civil status book the divorce or annulment of marriage performed abroad by the individual.\n3. In accordance with clause 1 of Article 33 of this Law, the district-level People's Committee of the place of residence of the person responsible for death registration shall record in the civil status book the divorce or annulment of marriage performed by a foreign authority."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 48"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 49,
        "text": {
          "en": "Procedure for recording in the civil status book of birth, guardianship, adoption, parent-child relationship, change of civil status and death\n1. To apply for the recording in the civil status book of information on birth, guardianship, adoption, parent-child relationship, change of civil status or death, a copy of the certificate capable of showing that these matters have been performed by a foreign authority, together with the form set by the prescribed template, must be submitted to the civil status registration agency.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, after receiving the certificates in full, the civil status officer shall carry out verification and, if they are in accordance with regulations, shall record the information in the civil status book, and the Justice Office shall report to the Chairman of the district-level People's Committee to issue an extract to the applicant.\nWhere verification is required, the processing time limit shall not exceed 3 working days."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 49"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 50,
        "text": {
          "en": "Procedure for recording in the civil status book of marriage, divorce and annulment of marriage\n1. To apply for the recording in the civil status book of information on marriage, divorce or annulment of marriage, a copy of the certificate capable of showing that these matters have been performed by a foreign authority, together with the form set by the prescribed template, must be submitted to the civil status registration agency.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·16·\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, within 12 days from the date of receipt of the dossier in full, if the conditions provided by law are satisfied, the civil status officer shall record the information in the civil status book, and the Justice Office shall report to the Chairman of the district-level People's Committee to issue an extract to the applicant.\n3. The Government has made specific provision on the procedures, formalities and time limits for the implementation of this Article by the various agencies.\nSection 7 DEATH REGISTRATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 50"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 51,
        "text": {
          "en": "Competence for death registration\n1. The district-level People's Committee of the last place of residence of the deceased shall carry out death registration for foreigners residing in Vietnam or for Vietnamese citizens.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, where the last place of residence of the deceased cannot be determined, the death registration shall be carried out by the district-level People's Committee of the place where the person died or where the body was found."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 51"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 52,
        "text": {
          "en": "Death registration procedure\n1. The applicant for death registration shall submit to the civil status registration agency the form set by the prescribed template and the notice of death or another certificate in place of the notice of death.\n2. In accordance with clause 1 of this Article, after receiving the certificates in full, if the application for death registration is true, the judicial-civil status officer shall register the death information in the civil status book and sign it together with the applicant, and the Justice Office shall report to the Chairman of the district-level People's Committee to issue an extract.\nWhere verification is required, the processing time limit shall not exceed 3 working days.\n3. After registering the death, the district-level People's Committee must report to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs in the form of a document accompanied by a civil status extract, and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs shall in turn notify the competent authority of the country of which the deceased was a citizen.\nWhere the deceased was a Vietnamese citizen settled abroad, the civil status officer shall lock the civil status information of the deceased in the electronic civil status database.\nChapter IV CIVIL STATUS REGISTRATION BY REPRESENTATIVE MISSIONS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 52"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 53,
        "text": {
          "en": "Civil status registration for Vietnamese citizens residing abroad\n1. In accordance with Article 3 of this Law, where the registration does not contravene the law of the receiving country or an international treaty to which Vietnam is a party, the representative mission shall carry out civil status registration for Vietnamese citizens residing abroad.\n2. In accordance with this Law, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs shall coordinate with the Ministry of Justice in conveying to representative missions the competence and procedures for civil status registration and for applying for civil status extracts on behalf of Vietnamese citizens residing abroad."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 53"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 54,
        "text": {
          "en": "Civil status officers of representative missions\n1. The representative mission shall designate counsellors and consuls to carry out civil status registration for Vietnamese citizens residing abroad.\n·17·\n2. A counsellor or consul responsible for civil status work must, in addition to meeting the conditions and requirements of his or her position, undergo professional training in civil status registration before carrying out civil status registration work."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 54"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 55,
        "text": {
          "en": "Compilation of the civil status book by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs The Ministry of Foreign Affairs shall compile the civil status book in order to record fully, update and manage uniformly the civil status information of Vietnamese citizens residing abroad registered at representative missions, and to serve as the basis for the issue of copies of civil status extracts."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 55"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 56,
        "text": {
          "en": "Reporting responsibility of representative missions After registering civil status for Vietnamese citizens residing abroad, the representative mission has the responsibility to report to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, in the form of a document accompanied by a copy of the civil status extract, so that it may be recorded in the civil status book and the information in the electronic civil status database may be promptly updated.\nChapter V CIVIL STATUS DATABASE AND ISSUE OF CIVIL STATUS EXTRACTS Section 1 CIVIL STATUS DATABASE"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 56"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 57,
        "text": {
          "en": "Civil status database\n1. The civil status database is State property, retains the civil status information of all persons, constitutes the basis for guaranteeing the lawful rights and interests of individuals, and serves information lookup and management and the formulation of the State's socio-economic development policies.\n2. The civil status database comprises the civil status book and the electronic civil status database and is the basis for the issue of civil status extracts."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 57"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 58,
        "text": {
          "en": "Civil status book\n1. The civil status book is the legal basis for editing, updating and adjusting a person's civil status information in the electronic civil status database.\nEach type of civil status information must be registered in one book, every page of which must be sealed across the seam; the civil status book shall be preserved permanently in accordance with law.\n2. The closing of the civil status book shall be carried out on the last day of each year, and at the closing the total number of pages and the total volume of information registered must be clearly recorded and signed and sealed by the head of the civil status registration agency.\nThe certificates, articles or other evidence submitted upon civil status registration must be preserved and managed in accordance with the law on archives.\n3. The civil status registration agency has the responsibility to preserve and manage the civil status book, the certificates, the articles and other evidence relating to civil status registration."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 58"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 59,
        "text": {
          "en": "Electronic civil status database\n1. The electronic civil status database shall be built in order to preserve, update, manage and look up civil status information, to serve the demand for online civil status registration and to supply and transmit the basic civil status information of individuals to the national population database.\n2. The Government has provided for the following: the coordination among ministries, ministerial-level agencies and People's Committees at all levels in managing and using the electronic civil status database; the procedures and formalities for online civil status registration; and the connection and exchange of information between the electronic civil status database and the national population database.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·18·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 59"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 60,
        "text": {
          "en": "Updating and adjustment of personal civil status information in the electronic civil status database\n1. After a person's civil status information has been registered in the civil status book, it must be promptly, fully and accurately registered in the electronic civil status database; the civil status registration agency is responsible for all civil status information updated in the electronic civil status database.\n2. Where the information in the electronic civil status database or in the national population database differs from the information in the civil status book, it must be adjusted so as to conform to the information in the civil status book."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 60"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 61,
        "text": {
          "en": "Principles of the management and use of the civil status database\n1. In accordance with law, the civil status database shall be preserved and managed confidentially so as to ensure its security, and only competent agencies, organisations and individuals may have access to and use it.\n2. The agencies managing the civil status database have the responsibility to keep confidential the personal information in the civil status database.\nSection 2 ISSUE OF CIVIL STATUS EXTRACTS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 61"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 62,
        "text": {
          "en": "Issue of the original civil status extract upon civil status registration\n1. Upon civil status registration, except for birth registration and marriage registration, the civil status registration agency shall issue one original civil status extract to the applicant.\n2. The original civil status extract may be certified by copy."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 62"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 63,
        "text": {
          "en": "Issue of copies of civil status extracts for civil status information already registered A person who moves from place to place has the right to request the agency managing the civil status database to issue copies of civil status extracts for civil status information already registered."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 63"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 64,
        "text": {
          "en": "Procedure for the issue of copies of civil status extracts\n1. The person concerned shall apply in person, or through a representative, to the agency managing the civil status database by submitting the form set by the prescribed template.\nWhere a competent agency or organisation requests the issue of copies of an individual's civil status extract, it shall submit to the agency managing the civil status database an application document clearly stating the reasons.\n2. Upon receipt of the application, if the conditions are satisfied, the agency managing the civil status database shall issue copies of the civil status extract to the applicant.\nChapter VI RESPONSIBILITIES OF THE STATE FOR THE MANAGEMENT OF CIVIL STATUS AND CIVIL STATUS OFFICERS Section 1 RESPONSIBILITIES OF THE STATE FOR THE MANAGEMENT OF CIVIL STATUS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 64"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 65,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of the Government\n1. The Government shall exercise unified State management of civil status, and the Ministry of Justice shall assist the Government in the State management of civil status.\n2. The contents of the State management of civil status comprise:\n(a) promulgating or submitting to the competent authority for promulgation legal normative documents on civil status registration and management, and organising their implementation;\n·19· (b) applying information technology in the registration and management of civil status and building and managing the electronic civil status database;\n(c) inspecting and settling complaints and denunciations and handling violations of law in the registration and management of civil status;\n(d) civil status statistics;\n(đ) international cooperation on civil status."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 65"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 66,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of the Ministry of Justice The Ministry of Justice shall manage civil status at the national level, with the following tasks and powers:\n1. To make provision on, guide and train civil status officers in the country in civil status registration and management work.\n2. To issue and guide the use and management of the civil status book, birth certificates, marriage certificates, civil status extracts and other civil status forms, and to make specific provision on the conditions and procedures for the registration of birth, marriage and death by persons who move from place to place.\n3. To build and manage uniformly the electronic civil status database, to guide local agencies in managing and using the civil status registration and management software and the electronic civil status database, and to supply the basic civil status information of individuals to the national population database.\n4. To consolidate annually the civil status situation and, after analysis, evaluation and statistics, to report to the Government."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 66"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 67,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs\n1. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs shall coordinate with the Ministry of Justice in the State management of civil status in respect of representative missions, with the following tasks and powers:\n(a) to make provision on, guide and inspect the civil status registration and management work of representative missions;\n(b) to organise professional training in civil status work for counsellors and consuls;\n(c) to compile the civil status book in order to manage the civil status information of Vietnamese citizens registered at representative missions;\n(d) to update and use the electronic civil status database and to issue copies of civil status extracts in accordance with law;\n(đ) to consolidate the civil status situation and compile the civil status information of representative missions and report to the Ministry of Justice in accordance with the regulations of the Government.\n2. Representative missions shall exercise State management of civil status over Vietnamese citizens residing abroad, with the following tasks and powers:\n(a) to carry out civil status registration for Vietnamese citizens residing abroad in accordance with the relevant law on civil status, the law on consular affairs and international treaties;\n(b) to assign civil status registration and management tasks at the representative mission to counsellors and consuls;\n(c) to manage and use the civil status book and civil status forms in accordance with regulations;\n(d) to manage, update and use the electronic civil status database and to issue copies of civil status extracts in accordance with regulations;\n(đ) to report the contents of civil status registration to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs for updating in the civil status database;\n(e) to consolidate the civil status situation and carry out civil status statistics and report to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs in accordance with regulations;\n(g) to preserve the certificates, articles and other evidence relating to civil status registration;\n(h) to settle, in accordance with their competence, complaints and denunciations and the violations of law arising in civil status work.\nIn accordance with points (a), (c), (d), đ, (e) and (g) of clause 2 of this Article, the counsellors and consuls responsible for civil status work have the responsibility to assist representative missions in performing their tasks and exercising their powers.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·20·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 67"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 68,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of the Ministry of Public Security The Ministry of Public Security shall take charge and coordinate with the Ministry of Justice, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and the various ministries in performing and exercising the following tasks and powers:\n1. to ensure, in accordance with law, the connectivity between the national population database and the electronic civil status database;\n2. to ensure the security of information in the electronic civil status database;\n3. to perform other tasks relating to civil status work."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 68"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 69,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of provincial People's Committees and of the People's Committees of centrally run cities\n1. Provincial People's Committees and the People's Committees of centrally run cities (hereinafter referred to as \"provincial People's Committees\") shall be responsible for the local State management of civil status, with the following tasks and powers:\n(a) to direct the organisation and implementation of civil status registration and management work;\n(b) to organise the dissemination and popularisation of the relevant law on civil status;\n(c) to make provision, in accordance with the regulations of the Government, on the allocation of civil status officers at district and commune level, and to provide material conditions for civil status registration and management work;\n(d) to manage, update and use the electronic civil status database in accordance with regulations;\n(đ) to inspect, in accordance with their competence, and to settle complaints and denunciations and the violations of law arising in civil status work;\n(e) to withdraw and destroy, except in the case of an unlawful marriage, civil status certificates issued by district-level People's Committees in breach of this Law;\n(g) to organise regular professional training in civil status work for civil status officers;\n(h) to consolidate the civil status situation, carry out civil status statistics and report to the Ministry of Justice in accordance with regulations.\n2. The provincial Department of Justice shall assist the provincial People's Committee in performing the tasks and exercising the powers conferred by points (a), (b), (d), đ, (g) and (h) of clause 1 of this Article.\n3. The Chairman of the provincial People's Committee shall be responsible for civil status registration and management work and for violations of law arising in that work as a result of negligent management."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 69"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 70,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of district-level People's Committees\n1. District-level People's Committees shall be responsible for the local State management of civil status, with the following tasks and powers:\n(a) to carry out civil status registration in accordance with this Law;\n(b) to direct and inspect the civil status registration and management work of commune-level People's Committees;\n(c) to organise the dissemination and popularisation of the relevant legal provisions on civil status;\n(d) to manage and use the civil status book and civil status forms in accordance with regulations;\n(đ) to allocate civil status officers in accordance with the regulations of the provincial People's Committee;\n(e) to manage, update and use the electronic civil status database and to issue copies of civil status extracts in accordance with regulations;\n(g) to inspect, in accordance with their competence, and to settle complaints and denunciations and the violations of law arising in civil status work;\n(h) to withdraw and destroy, except in the case of an unlawful marriage, civil status certificates issued by commune-level People's Committees in breach of this Law;\n·21· (i) to consolidate the civil status situation, carry out civil status statistics and report to the provincial People's Committee in accordance with the regulations of the Government;\n(k) to preserve the civil status book and the civil status registration dossiers.\n2. The Justice Office shall assist the district-level People's Committee in performing the tasks and exercising the powers conferred by points (a), (b), (c), (d), (e), (g), (i) and (k) of clause 1 of this Article.\n3. In accordance with this Law"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 70"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 71,
        "text": {
          "en": "for a district-level administrative unit in which no commune-level administrative unit has been established, it shall, in addition to performing the tasks and exercising the powers of a district-level People's Committee in civil status registration and management, also perform the tasks and exercise the powers of a commune-level People's Committee in civil status registration and management.\n4. The Chairman of the district-level People's Committee shall be responsible for civil status registration and management work and for violations of law arising in that work as a result of negligent management.\nArticle 71  Responsibilities of commune-level People's Committees\n1. Commune-level People's Committees shall be responsible for the local State management of civil status, with the following tasks and powers:\n(a) to carry out civil status registration in accordance with this Law;\n(b) to allocate, in accordance with the regulations of the higher-level People's Committee, judicial-civil status officers to carry out civil status registration;\n(c) to disseminate and popularise the relevant law on civil status;\n(d) to manage and use the civil status book and civil status forms in accordance with regulations;\n(đ) to manage, update and use the electronic civil status database and to issue copies of civil status extracts in accordance with regulations;\n(e) to consolidate the civil status situation, carry out civil status statistics and report to the district-level People's Committee in accordance with the regulations of the Government;\n(g) to preserve the civil status book and the civil status registration dossiers;\n(h) to settle, in accordance with their competence, complaints and denunciations and the violations of law arising in civil status work.\n2. The Chairman of the commune-level People's Committee shall regularly direct and urge birth and death registration and shall be responsible for civil status registration and management work and for violations of law arising in that work as a result of negligent management.\nCommune-level judicial-civil status officers shall perform the tasks and exercise the powers conferred by points (a), (c), (d), đ, (e) and (g) of clause 1 of this Article.\nSection 2 CIVIL STATUS OFFICERS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 71"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 72,
        "text": {
          "en": "Civil status officers\n1. Civil status officers comprise commune-level judicial-civil status officers, civil status officers of the Justice Offices under district-level People's Committees, and the counsellors and consuls responsible for civil status work in representative missions.\n2. A commune-level judicial-civil status officer must meet the following standards:\n(a) to hold at least an intermediate qualification in law and to have undergone professional training in civil status work;\n(b) to have clear handwriting and a level of computer skills appropriate to the requirements of the work.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·22·\n3. A civil status officer of a Justice Office must hold at least a bachelor's degree in law and must have undergone professional training in civil status work.\n4. Counsellors and consuls responsible for civil status work in representative missions must have undergone professional training in civil status work."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 72"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 73,
        "text": {
          "en": "Tasks and powers of civil status officers\n1. In civil status work, a commune-level judicial-civil status officer has the following tasks and powers:\n(a) to comply with the provisions of this Law and other legal provisions relating to civil status;\n(b) to be responsible before the commune-level People's Committee and to bear legal responsibility for civil status registration;\n(c) to disseminate and popularise the law and to mobilise the people to comply with the legal provisions on civil status;\n(d) to assist the commune-level People's Committee in registering civil status promptly, accurately, objectively and truthfully and in fully updating the civil status information registered in the electronic civil status database;\n(đ) to carry out reviews proactively so that births and deaths within their jurisdiction may be registered promptly;\nfor areas within their jurisdiction where the population is dispersed, travel is difficult and the distance from the office of the commune-level People's Committee is considerable, the judicial-civil status officer shall report to the commune-level People's Committee and the latter shall organise mobile registration of birth, marriage and death;\n(e) to keep improving their legal knowledge in order to enhance their capacity in civil status registration work and to participate in the various specialised professional training courses organised by the People's Committee or the higher-level judicial body;\n(g) to report proactively to, and to propose to, the People's Committee at the same level that it coordinate with the various agencies and organisations in reviewing and confirming civil status information; to request the various agencies, organisations and individuals to provide verification information when civil status registration is carried out; and to coordinate with the public security agencies in jointly supplying the basic civil status information of individuals to the national population database.\n2. Within the scope of their own tasks and powers, the provisions of clause 1 of this Article also apply to civil status officers of the Justice Offices and to the counsellors and consuls responsible for civil status work in representative missions."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 73"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 74,
        "text": {
          "en": "Prohibited acts of civil status officers\n1. Acting in a domineering manner, adopting a bureaucratic tone, extorting, delaying, creating difficulties or accepting bribes in carrying out civil status registration and management.\n2. Collecting civil status fees higher than the prescribed level in civil status registration.\n3. Prescribing formalities and certificates in breach of this Law, or deliberately prolonging the civil status registration time limit.\n4. Deleting, altering or making errors in the information in the civil status database.\n5. Registering and issuing certificates in breach of this Law.\n6. Disclosing the private personal information learned through civil status registration.\n7. In accordance with law, civil status officers who breach the provisions of this Article may, depending on the nature and seriousness of the violation, be subject to disciplinary measures or to examination of criminal liability.\n·23· Chapter VII IMPLEMENTATION PROVISIONS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 74"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 75,
        "text": {
          "en": "Value of civil status books and civil status certificates prepared and issued before the date on which this Law takes effect\n1. Civil status books retained from before the date on which this Law takes effect may still serve as the basis for certifying a person's civil status information, for looking up information and issuing civil status extracts, and for issuing forms confirming marital status.\n2. Civil status certificates issued to individuals before the date on which this Law takes effect in accordance with the relevant law on civil status shall retain their validity for use."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 75"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 76,
        "text": {
          "en": "Transitional provisions\n1. Civil status registration dossiers received but not yet fully processed before the date on which this Law takes effect shall continue to be processed in accordance with the law on civil status in force at the time of receipt.\n2. The Government shall make specific provision on the re-registration of births, deaths and marriages registered before the date on which this Law takes effect.\n3. The Government shall give guidance on the following:\nthe review of civil status work and the training and consolidation of the contingent of civil status officers so that the tasks and powers conferred by this Law may be performed and exercised; the provision on the procedures for birth and marriage registration; the issue of certificates of marital status to Vietnamese citizens; and the regime for preparing, managing and using civil status books during the transitional period before the completion of the national population database, so that uniform implementation throughout the country in accordance with this Law is achieved no later than 1 January 2020."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 76"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 77,
        "text": {
          "en": "Effect\n1. This Law takes effect on 1 January 2016.\n2. The Government and the competent agencies shall make specific provision on the articles of this Law.\nThis Law was adopted on 20 November 2014 at the eighth session of the Thirteenth National Assembly of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam.\nSigned by the Chairman of the National Assembly: Nguyen Sinh Hung Office of the President of the State Certified true copy Hanoi, 4 December 2014 (KT) Document No. of the Office of the President of the State: Copy of the Office of the President of the State No. 32 Deputy Director-General in charge Seal of the Office of the President of the State Giang Son"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 77"
        }
      }
    ]
  };

