  TR["VN-H072"] = {
    "lawId": "VN-H072",
    "sourceLang": "zh",
    "targetLang": "en",
    "engine": "llm",
    "model": "deepseek-v4.1-flash",
    "promptVersion": "calr-vi2en-v1",
    "translatedAt": "2026-10-28",
    "reviewed": "machine",
    "completenessNote": "逐条对译，共 64 条；条号与原文一一对应，未合并、未省略。",
    "articles": [
      {
        "no": 1,
        "text": {
          "en": "Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·60· The Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam No. 03/2007/QH12 of 21 November 2007 of the National Assembly, which took effect on 1 July 2008, as amended and supplemented by:\n1. The Law on Requisition and Acquisition of Property No. 15/2008/QH12 of 3 June 2008 of the National Assembly, which took effect on 1 January 2009;\n2. The Law No. 35/2018/QH14 of 20 November 2018 of the National Assembly Amending and Supplementing a Number of Articles of 37 Laws Relating to Planning, which took effect on 1 January 2019.\nPursuant to the 1992 Constitution of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam, as amended and supplemented by Resolution No. 51/2001/QH10, the National Assembly hereby promulgates the Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases.\nChapter I GENERAL PROVISIONS\nScope of regulation and subjects of application\n1. This Law provides for the prevention and control of infectious diseases, border medical quarantine, the fight against epidemics and the conditions for ensuring the prevention and control of infectious diseases in humans.\nThe prevention and control of acquired immune deficiency syndrome caused by the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV/AIDS) is not within the scope of regulation of this Law.\n2. This Law applies to domestic agencies, organisations and individuals and to foreign agencies, organisations and individuals in Vietnam."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 1"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 2,
        "text": {
          "en": "Interpretation of terms In this Law, the following terms shall be construed as follows:\n1. Infectious disease means a disease transmitted, directly or indirectly, from person to person or from animal to person by infectious disease pathogens.\n2. Infectious disease pathogen means a virus, bacterium, parasite or fungus capable of causing an infectious disease.\n3. Disease transmission vector means an insect, animal, environment, food or other object that carries an infectious disease pathogen and is capable of transmitting the disease.\n4. Infectious disease patient means a person infected with an infectious disease pathogen who exhibits symptoms of the disease.\n* Translator: Tao Wenwen, doctoral student at the School of Asian Studies, Beijing Foreign Studies University.\n·61· The Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam\n5. Infectious disease pathogen carrier means a person who carries an infectious disease pathogen but shows no symptom of the disease.\n6. Contact person means a person who has been in contact with an infectious disease patient, an infectious disease pathogen carrier or a disease transmission vector and who may be infected with the disease.\n7. Suspected infectious disease patient means a contact person or a person who exhibits symptoms of an infectious disease but whose pathogen is unidentified.\n8. Infectious disease surveillance means the continuous and systematic collection of information on the situation and trends of infectious diseases, and the analysis and interpretation thereof, so as to provide information for the formulation of plans as well as for the effective implementation and evaluation of infectious disease prevention and control measures.\n9. Biosafety of testing means the adoption of measures to reduce or eliminate the risk of an infectious disease pathogen spreading outwards from a testing facility to the environment and society.\n10. Vaccine means a preparation containing antigens which enables the body to produce an immune response and which is used for disease prevention.\n11. Medical biological product means a product of biological origin used for the prevention, treatment and diagnosis of human diseases.\n12. Immune status means the degree of resistance of an individual or a community to a given infectious disease pathogen.\n13. Epidemic means the occurrence of an infectious disease at a given place and within a defined period of time where the number of infected persons exceeds the number normally expected.\n14. Epidemic area means an area determined by a competent authority to be affected by an epidemic.\n15. Epidemic risk area means an area adjacent to an epidemic area or an area in which factors giving rise to an epidemic have appeared.\n16. Medical isolation means the separation of infectious disease patients, suspected infectious disease patients or infectious disease pathogen carriers, or of objects that may carry infectious disease pathogens, so as to restrict the spread of the disease.\n17. Medical treatment means measures using vaccines, medical biological products, medical isolation, disinfection and destruction of infectious disease pathogens and disease transmission vectors, and other medical measures."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 2"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 3,
        "text": {
          "en": "Classification of infectious diseases\n1. Infectious diseases comprise the following groups:\n(1) Group A comprises exceptionally dangerous infectious diseases that are capable of rapid transmission and wide spread and that have a high mortality rate or an unidentified pathogen.\nGroup A infectious diseases comprise poliomyelitis, avian influenza A H5N1, plague, smallpox, haemorrhagic fever caused by the Ebola, Lassa or Marburg viruses, West Nile fever, yellow fever, cholera, and severe acute respiratory infection caused by viruses and by emerging dangerous infectious diseases with unidentified pathogens.\n(2) Group B comprises serious infectious diseases that are capable of rapid transmission and that cause death.\nGroup B infectious diseases comprise adenovirus disease, acquired immune deficiency syndrome caused by the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV/AIDS), diphtheria, influenza, rabies, pertussis, tuberculosis, human streptococcus suis infection, amoebic dysentery, bacillary dysentery, mumps, dengue fever, dengue haemorrhagic fever, malaria, typhus, measles, hand-foot-mouth disease, anthrax, chickenpox, typhoid, tetanus, rubella, viral hepatitis, meningitis Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·62· caused by meningococcus, viral encephalitis, spirochaetal jaundice and rotavirus diarrhoea.\n(3) Group C comprises infectious diseases of low danger and slow transmission capacity.\nGroup C infectious diseases comprise: chlamydia, syphilis, helminthiasis, gonorrhoea, trachoma, Candida albicans infection, nocardiosis, leprosy, cytomegalovirus disease, herpes, tapeworm infection, fascioliasis, paragonimiasis, intestinal fluke infection, scrub typhus, rickettsial fever, Hanta haemorrhagic fever, trichomoniasis, contagious pustular dermatitis, pharyngitis, stomatitis and myocarditis caused by Coxsackie virus, enteritis caused by Giardia, enteritis caused by Vibrio parahaemolyticus and other infectious diseases.\n2. The Minister of Health shall decide on the adjustment and supplementation of the lists of infectious diseases of each group provided for in clause 1 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 3"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 4,
        "text": {
          "en": "Principles of prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. Disease prevention shall be taken as the principal approach, in which notification, education, mass communication and surveillance of infectious diseases constitute the main measures. Specialised medical and technical measures shall be combined with social and administrative measures in the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n2. Intersectoral coordination and social mobilisation shall be carried out in the prevention and control of infectious diseases, and activities for the prevention and control of infectious diseases shall be incorporated into socio-economic development plans.\n3. Information on epidemics shall be notified in an open, accurate and timely manner.\n4. Epidemic prevention and control activities shall be carried out proactively, actively, promptly and thoroughly."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 4"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 5,
        "text": {
          "en": "The State's policy on prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. Priority shall be given to, and support provided for, training in the preventive medicine speciality.\n2. Priority shall be given to investment in improving the capacity of the contingent of officials and in the system for detecting and monitoring infectious diseases and in research on and the production of vaccines and medical biological products.\n3. Support shall be provided for, and scientific research, exchange of experts, training and technology transfer in the field of prevention and control of infectious diseases shall be encouraged.\n4. Support shall be provided for the treatment and nursing of patients infected with infectious diseases as a result of occupational risks and in other necessary cases.\n5. Support shall be provided for losses incurred as a result of the destruction, in accordance with law, of livestock and poultry carrying infectious disease pathogens.\n6. The whole society shall be mobilised to make financial, technical and human resources contributions to the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n7. Cooperation with international organisations, regional countries and countries throughout the world in the prevention and control of infectious diseases shall be expanded."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 5"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 6,
        "text": {
          "en": "State management of the prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. The Government shall exercise unified management of the prevention and control of infectious diseases throughout the country.\n2. The Ministry of Health shall be responsible to the Government for exercising State management of the prevention and control of infectious diseases throughout the country.\n3. Ministries, ministerial-level agencies shall, within the scope of their functions and powers, be responsible for coordinating with the Ministry of Health in exercising State management of the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n4. People's Committees at all levels shall exercise State management of the prevention and control of infectious diseases in accordance with the decentralisation established by the Government.\n·63· The Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 6"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 7,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of agencies, organisations and individuals in the prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. Agencies, organisations and people's armed units shall, within the scope of their assigned functions and powers, be responsible for formulating and organising the implementation of plans for the prevention and control of infectious diseases; when an epidemic occurs, they shall closely coordinate with and assist one another and shall comply with and implement the direction and mobilisation of the steering committee for the fight against the epidemic.\n2. The Vietnam Fatherland Front and its member organisations shall be responsible for disseminating information and mobilising the people to participate in the prevention and control of infectious diseases and to participate in supervising the implementation of the law on the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n3. Domestic agencies, organisations and individuals and foreign agencies, organisations and individuals in Vietnam shall be responsible for participating in the prevention and control of infectious diseases in accordance with this Law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 7"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 8,
        "text": {
          "en": "Prohibited acts\n1. Deliberately spreading infectious disease pathogens.\n2. Infectious disease patients, suspected infectious disease patients and infectious disease pathogen carriers performing work that is likely to spread infectious disease pathogens as provided for by law.\n3. Concealing, failing to report or failing to report in a timely manner cases of infection with an infectious disease as provided for by law.\n4. Deliberately making false reports and notifications concerning an infectious disease.\n5. Discriminating against infectious disease patients, or circulating negative images and information relating to infectious disease patients.\n6. Failing to carry out or failing to carry out in a timely manner the measures for the prevention and control of infectious diseases provided for in this Law.\n7. Failing to implement measures for the prevention and control of infectious diseases as required by competent agencies and organisations.\nChapter II PREVENTION OF INFECTIOUS DISEASES Section 1 NOTIFICATION, EDUCATION AND MASS COMMUNICATION ON THE PREVENTION AND CONTROL OF INFECTIOUS DISEASES"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 8"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 9,
        "text": {
          "en": "Content of notification, education and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. The line and guidelines of the Party and the policies and laws of the State on the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n2. Causes of diseases, channels of transmission, methods of identification and measures for the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n3. Consequences of infectious diseases for human health and life and for the socio-economic development of the country.\n4. Responsibilities of agencies, organisations and individuals in the prevention and control of infectious diseases."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 9"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 10,
        "text": {
          "en": "Targets of notification, education and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. Everyone may receive notification, education and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n2. Infectious disease patients, suspected infectious disease patients, infectious disease pathogen carriers and members of their families, and persons in epidemic areas and epidemic risk areas, may receive notification, education and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases on a priority basis.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·64·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 10"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 11,
        "text": {
          "en": "Requirements for notification, education and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. Accurate, clear, easy to understand, practical and timely.\n2. Appropriate to the target audience, cultural traditions, ethnic groups, social morality, religions, beliefs and customs."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 11"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 12,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities for notification, education and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. Agencies, organisations and people's armed units shall, within the scope of their functions and powers, be responsible for carrying out notification, education and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n2. The Ministry of Health shall be responsible for presiding over and coordinating with the relevant agencies in providing accurate and timely information on infectious diseases.\n3. The Ministry of Information and Communications shall be responsible for directing mass media agencies to carry out notification and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases on a regular basis and to incorporate programmes on the prevention and control of infectious diseases into other notification and mass communication programmes.\n4. The Ministry of Education and Training shall be responsible for presiding over and coordinating with the Ministry of Health, the Ministry of Labour, Invalids and Social Affairs and the relevant ministries and ministerial-level agencies in formulating content on the prevention and control of infectious diseases for integration into other educational content.\n5. People's Committees at all levels shall be responsible for directing and organising the implementation of notification, education and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases for the local population.\n6. Mass media agencies shall be responsible, in accordance with the regulations of the Ministry of Information and Communications, for giving priority to broadcasting time and duration for notification, education and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases on radio and television, and for giving priority to space and position for such content in the print press, visual press and electronic press. Notification, education and mass communication on the prevention and control of infectious diseases through the mass media shall be provided free of charge, except where they are carried out under separate contracts for festivals or are sponsored by domestic or foreign organisations and individuals.\nSection 2 HYGIENE FOR THE PREVENTION OF INFECTIOUS DISEASES"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 12"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 13,
        "text": {
          "en": "Hygiene for the prevention of infectious diseases in educational establishments under the national education system\n1. Educational establishments must be built on dry, clean and elevated ground, far from pollution, with an adequate supply of domestic water and sanitary facilities;\nclassrooms must be well ventilated and adequately lit; and food used in educational establishments must ensure safe hygienic quality.\n2. Educational establishments shall be responsible for educating learners on hygiene for the prevention of infectious diseases, including personal hygiene, hygiene in work and daily life and environmental hygiene.\n3. The medical units of educational establishments shall be responsible for disseminating information on hygiene for disease prevention, inspecting and monitoring environmental hygiene and food hygiene and safety, and carrying out measures for the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n4. The Minister of Health shall issue the national technical standards on hygiene for disease prevention in educational establishments provided for in clause 1 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 13"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 14,
        "text": {
          "en": "Hygiene in the supply of clean water and hygiene of domestic water sources\n1. Clean water must ensure conformity with the national technical standards prescribed by the Minister of Health.\n2. Clean water supply units shall be responsible for adopting technical measures, maintaining environmental hygiene and conducting self-inspection so as to ensure the quality of clean water.\n·65· The Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam\n3. State competent medical institutions shall be responsible for regularly inspecting the quality of clean water supplied by units and for inspecting the periodic health examinations of the staff of clean water supply units.\n4. People's Committees at all levels shall be responsible for organising the implementation of the protection and maintenance of hygiene and the prevention of pollution of domestic water sources, and for creating conditions for the supply of clean water.\n5. Agencies, organisations and individuals shall be responsible for protecting and maintaining the hygiene of, and preventing pollution of, domestic water sources."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 14"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 15,
        "text": {
          "en": "Hygiene in the rearing, transport, slaughter and destruction of livestock, poultry and other animals\n1. The rearing, transport, slaughter and destruction of livestock, poultry and other animals must ensure hygiene without polluting the environment or domestic water sources or spreading infectious disease pathogens.\n2. State competent veterinary institutions shall be responsible for guiding organisations and individuals in implementing hygiene measures in the rearing, transport, slaughter and destruction of livestock, poultry and other animals so as to prevent the transmission of diseases to humans."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 15"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 16,
        "text": {
          "en": "Food hygiene and safety\n1. Organisations and individuals engaged in the cultivation, rearing, harvesting, fishing, primary processing, processing, packaging, preservation, transport and trading of food shall be responsible for ensuring that food is not infected with infectious disease pathogens and for complying with the provisions of the law on food hygiene and safety.\n2. Consumers shall have the right to obtain information on food hygiene and safety and shall be responsible for ensuring food hygiene and safety, fully complying with guidance on food hygiene and safety, and reporting food poisoning and food-borne diseases.\n3. State competent food hygiene and safety institutions shall be responsible for guiding organisations and individuals in adopting measures to ensure food hygiene and safety for the prevention and control of infectious diseases."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 16"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 17,
        "text": {
          "en": "Construction hygiene\n1. National construction hygiene standards prescribed by the Minister of Health must be complied with in the construction of works.\n2. Investment projects for the construction of industrial parks, urban areas, concentrated residential areas and medical examination and treatment establishments for infectious diseases may be constructed only after the report on health impact assessment has been appraised by a competent medical institution.\n3. Medical examination and treatment establishments for infectious diseases and establishments at risk of transmitting infectious disease pathogens must maintain a safe environmental distance from residential areas and nature reserves in accordance with the regulations of the Minister of Health.\n4. Agencies, organisations and individuals shall be responsible for implementing matters relating to the assurance of construction hygiene."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 17"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 18,
        "text": {
          "en": "Hygiene in the keeping, embalming, burial and transfer of corpses and remains\n1. A deceased person must be buried within 48 hours after death, except where the corpse is preserved in accordance with the regulations of the Minister of Health; where a patient with an infectious disease or a person suspected of being a Group A infectious disease patient dies, the corpse must be sterilised and buried within 24 hours.\n2. The preservation, keeping, embalming, burial and transfer of corpses and remains shall be carried out in accordance with the regulations of the Minister of Health."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 18"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 19,
        "text": {
          "en": "Other activities in hygiene for the prevention of infectious diseases\n1. Agencies, organisations and individuals must implement measures to ensure hygiene in places of residence, public units, production and business establishments and means of transport, and measures for the treatment of industrial and domestic waste, as well as other measures provided for by law, so that infectious diseases do not arise and spread.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·66·\n2. Everyone shall be responsible for maintaining personal hygiene and preventing infectious diseases.\nSection 3 SURVEILLANCE OF INFECTIOUS DISEASES"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 19"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 20,
        "text": {
          "en": "Surveillance activities for infectious diseases\n1. Surveillance of cases of infection, suspected infection and pathogen carriage.\n2. Surveillance of infectious disease pathogens.\n3. Surveillance of disease transmission vectors."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 20"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 21,
        "text": {
          "en": "Content of surveillance of infectious diseases\n1. Surveillance of cases of infection, suspected infection and pathogen carriage comprises information on the principal characteristics of the place, time and number of cases of infection and death, the condition of the disease, the immune status and the number of persons, and other necessary information.\nIn emergency situations, competent medical institutions may take blood samples from suspected infectious disease patients for testing for surveillance purposes.\n2. Surveillance of infectious disease pathogens comprises information relating to the types, biological characteristics, sources of infection and modes of transmission.\n3. Surveillance of disease transmission vectors comprises information relating to the quantity, density, composition and degree of infection of disease transmission vectors with infectious disease pathogens."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 21"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 22,
        "text": {
          "en": "Reporting on surveillance of infectious diseases\n1. Reports on the surveillance of infectious diseases must be submitted to State competent medical institutions, and the content of such reports shall comprise the information provided for in Article 21 of this Law.\n2. Reports on the surveillance of infectious diseases must be made in writing; in emergency situations, reports may be made by fax, electronic mail, telegram, telephone or in person, and a written report shall be submitted within 24 hours.\n3. The reporting regime for the surveillance of infectious diseases comprises:\n(1) periodic reports;\n(2) rapid reports;\n(3) unscheduled reports.\n4. Upon receipt of a report, a State competent medical institution must process it and notify the reporting body.\n5. Where the existence of an epidemic is confirmed, the State competent medical institution must immediately report to the superior State competent medical institution and to the person competent to announce the epidemic.\n6. The Minister of Health shall make specific provisions on the regime for notification and reporting of infectious diseases."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 22"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 23,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities for surveillance of infectious diseases\n1. People's Committees at all levels shall direct and organise the implementation of local surveillance of infectious diseases.\n2. State competent medical institutions shall be responsible for assisting People's Committees at the same level in guiding medical units in carrying out surveillance of infectious diseases.\n3. Medical units shall be responsible for carrying out surveillance of infectious diseases. Upon detecting an environment containing Group A infectious disease pathogens, a Group A infectious Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·67· The Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam disease patient, a person suspected of being a Group A infectious disease patient or a Group A infectious disease pathogen carrier, the medical unit must notify the State competent medical institution and carry out cleaning, sterilisation, disinfection and other measures for the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n4. Where agencies, organisations or individuals detect a disease or signs of an infectious disease, they shall be responsible for notifying the People's Committee, a specialised medical institution or the nearest medical unit.\n5. In the course of surveillance of infectious diseases, testing units shall be responsible for conducting tests as required by competent medical institutions.\n6. The Minister of Health shall issue the specialised technical regulations on surveillance of infectious diseases.\n7. The Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development, the Ministry of Natural Resources and Environment and other ministries and ministerial-level agencies shall, in the course of performing their tasks and exercising their powers within the scope of their respective State management, be responsible for coordinating with the Ministry of Health in surveillance activities upon detecting infectious disease pathogens.\nSection 4 BIOSAFETY OF TESTING"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 23"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 24,
        "text": {
          "en": "Assurance of biosafety of testing laboratories\n1. Testing laboratories must ensure conformity with the biosafety conditions of each level and may conduct testing only after having been granted a certificate of conformity with biosafety standards by a State competent medical institution.\n2. The Government shall make specific provisions on the assurance of biosafety of testing laboratories."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 24"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 25,
        "text": {
          "en": "Management of disease specimens\n1. The collection, transport, preservation, storage, use, study, exchange and destruction of disease specimens relating to infectious disease patients must comply with the regulations on the regime for the management of disease specimens.\n2. Only units with adequate conditions may keep, store, use, study, exchange and destroy disease specimens of Group A infectious diseases.\n3. The Minister of Health shall make specific provisions on the regime for the management of disease specimens provided for in clauses 1 and 2 of this Article and on the conditions to be satisfied by units managing disease specimens."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 25"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 26,
        "text": {
          "en": "Protection of laboratory staff\n1. Laboratory staff exposed to infectious disease pathogens must receive training in specialised knowledge and operational skills and must be equipped with personal protective equipment to prevent infection with pathogens.\n2. Laboratory staff exposed to infectious disease pathogens must comply with specialised technical procedures for testing.\nSection 5 USE OF VACCINES AND MEDICAL BIOLOGICAL PRODUCTS FOR DISEASE PREVENTION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 26"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 27,
        "text": {
          "en": "Principles of the use of vaccines and medical biological products\n1. Vaccines and medical biological products used must satisfy the conditions provided for in Article 36 of the Law on Pharmacy.\n2. The use of vaccines and medical biological products shall follow either a voluntary or a compulsory form.\n3. Vaccines and medical biological products must be used for the correct purposes, on the correct subjects, at the correct time, of the correct types and in accordance with the technical procedures for use.\n4. Vaccines and medical biological products must be used in medical units with adequate conditions.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·68·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 27"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 28,
        "text": {
          "en": "Voluntary use of vaccines and medical biological products\n1. Everyone has the right to use vaccines and medical biological products to protect his or her own health and the health of the community.\n2. The State shall support and encourage citizens to use vaccines and medical biological products on a voluntary basis.\n3. Physicians and medical staff directly involved in the nursing and treatment of infectious disease patients, and laboratory staff exposed to infectious disease pathogens, may use vaccines and medical biological products free of charge."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 28"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 29,
        "text": {
          "en": "Compulsory use of vaccines and medical biological products\n1. Persons in epidemic areas and persons travelling to epidemic areas who are at risk of infection with an infectious disease must use vaccines and medical biological products against diseases for which vaccines and medical biological products are available.\n2. Minors and pregnant women must use vaccines and medical biological products against the infectious diseases covered by the Expanded Immunisation Programme.\n3. Parents or guardians of minors and all citizens shall be responsible for complying with the requirements of competent medical units in the compulsory use of vaccines and medical biological products.\n4. The following persons shall use vaccines and medical biological products compulsorily and free of charge:\n(1) persons in epidemic areas who are at risk of infection with an infectious disease;\n(2) persons sent by State competent agencies to epidemic areas;\n(3) the subjects provided for in clause 2 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 29"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 30,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities for organising the use of vaccines and medical biological products\n1. The State shall be responsible for ensuring funding for the use of the vaccines and medical biological products provided for in clause 3 of Article 28 and clause 4 of Article 29 of this Law.\n2. The Minister of Health shall be responsible for:\n(1) issuing the list of infectious diseases against which the use of vaccines and medical biological products is compulsory as provided for in clause 1 of Article 29 of this Law;\n(2) organising the implementation of the Expanded Immunisation Programme and prescribing the list of infectious diseases against which the use of vaccines and medical biological products is compulsory and the age groups of minors covered by the Expanded Immunisation Programme as provided for in clause 2 of Article 29 of this Law;\n(3) prescribing the scope and subjects of compulsory use of vaccines and medical biological products according to the epidemic situation;\n(4) prescribing the use of vaccines and medical biological products provided for in clause 3 of Article 27 and the conditions to be satisfied by medical units provided for in clause 4 of Article 27 of this Law;\n(5) prescribing the establishment, organisation and operation of the professional advisory council and the examination of causes upon the occurrence of incidents in the course of the use of vaccines and medical biological products provided for in clauses 5 and 6 of this Article.\n3. People's Committees of provinces and centrally-run cities (hereinafter referred to as provincial People's Committees) shall be responsible for directing and organising the implementation of vaccination and the use of vaccines and medical biological products.\n·69· The Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam\n4. Medical units shall be responsible for vaccinating and using vaccines and medical biological products within their professional scope in accordance with the regulations of the Minister of Health.\n5. Organisations and individuals producing, trading in and preserving vaccines and medical biological products, and staff carrying out vaccination and using vaccines and medical biological products, that commit faults in the course of the production, trading and preservation of vaccines and medical biological products shall bear responsibility in accordance with law for violations causing incidents to users of vaccines and medical biological products.\n6. Where an incident seriously affecting the health of a vaccinated person or endangering his or her life occurs in the course of expanded immunisation, the State shall be responsible for compensating the vaccinated person. Where the fault is determined to have been caused by an organisation or individual producing, trading in or preserving vaccines and medical biological products, or by a staff member carrying out vaccination, such organisation or individual must reimburse the State in accordance with law.\nSection 6 PREVENTION OF INFECTIOUS DISEASES IN MEDICAL EXAMINATION AND TREATMENT ESTABLISHMENTS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 30"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 31,
        "text": {
          "en": "Measures for the prevention of infectious diseases in medical examination and treatment establishments\n1. Isolation of infectious disease patients.\n2. Sterilisation and disinfection of the environment and treatment of waste from medical examination and treatment establishments.\n3. Personal protection and personal hygiene.\n4. Other specialised measures provided for by law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 31"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 32,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of medical examination and treatment establishments in the prevention of infectious diseases\n1. To apply isolation measures appropriate to each type of disease and to provide comprehensive nursing care to infectious disease patients. Where a patient fails to comply with the isolation requirements of the medical examination and treatment establishment, compulsory isolation measures shall be applied to him or her in accordance with the regulations of the Government.\n2. To organise the implementation of measures for the sterilisation and disinfection of the environment and the treatment of waste from the medical examination and treatment establishment.\n3. To ensure conditions for protective clothing and personal hygiene for physicians, medical staff, patients and their family members.\n4. To carry out health observation of physicians and medical staff directly involved in the nursing and treatment of Group A infectious disease patients.\n5. To notify the medical prophylaxis institution at the same level of information relating to infectious disease patients.\n6. To implement other specialised measures provided for by law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 32"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 33,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of physicians and medical staff in the prevention of infectious diseases in medical treatment establishments\n1. To implement the measures for the prevention of infectious diseases provided for in Article 31 of this Law.\n2. To explain measures for the prevention of infectious diseases to patients and their family members.\n3. To keep information on patients confidential."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 33"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 34,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities of patients and their family members in the prevention of infectious diseases in medical examination and treatment establishments\n1. A patient shall be responsible for:\n(1) truthfully reporting changes in his or her condition;\n(2) complying with the instructions and guidance of physicians and medical staff and with the internal rules and regulations of the medical examination and treatment establishment;\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·70·\n(3) after being discharged from hospital, a Group A infectious disease patient must immediately register for health observation with the medical unit of the commune, ward or township of his or her place of residence.\n2. The family members of a patient shall be responsible for complying with the instructions and guidance of physicians and medical staff and with the internal rules and regulations of the medical examination and treatment establishment.\nChapter III BORDER MEDICAL QUARANTINE"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 34"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 35,
        "text": {
          "en": "Subjects and locations of border medical quarantine\n1. The subjects subject to border medical quarantine comprise:\n(1) persons entering, leaving or transiting Vietnam;\n(2) means of transport entering, leaving or transiting Vietnam;\n(3) goods imported into, exported from or transiting Vietnam;\n(4) corpses, remains, medical microbiological specimens, cell tissues and human organs transported across the border of Vietnam.\n2. Border medical quarantine shall be carried out at border gates."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 35"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 36,
        "text": {
          "en": "Content of border medical quarantine\n1. The subjects subject to border medical inspection provided for in clause 1 of Article 35 of this Law must make medical declarations.\n2. Medical inspection comprises the inspection of medical documents and physical inspection. Physical inspection shall be carried out where the inspected subject departs from or passes through an epidemic area or is suspected of being infected with a disease or of carrying an infectious disease pathogen.\n3. Medical treatment shall be carried out where medical inspection has been conducted and signs of carriage of Group A infectious disease pathogens are detected in a subject of medical quarantine. Where a declaration is received from the owner of a means of transport or where there is clear evidence that a means of transport, its passengers or its cargo show signs of carrying Group A infectious disease pathogens, such means of transport together with the passengers and cargo carried thereon must be isolated for medical inspection before the procedures for entry, import or transit are carried out; where the isolation requirements of the border medical quarantine unit are not complied with, compulsory isolation measures shall be applied.\n4. Surveillance of infectious diseases shall be carried out in border gate areas in accordance with the provisions of Section 3 of Chapter II of this Law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 36"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 37,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities for carrying out border medical quarantine\n1. The subjects provided for in point (1) of clause 1 of Article 35 of this Law, and the owners or managers of the means of transport provided for in points (2), (3) and\n(4) of clause 1 of Article 35, must make medical declarations, implement medical surveillance, inspection and treatment measures and pay medical quarantine fees in accordance with law.\n2. Border medical quarantine units shall be responsible for organising the implementation of the content of medical quarantine provided for in Article 36 of this Law and for issuing certificates of medical treatment.\n3. Functional agencies at border gates shall be responsible for coordinating with border medical quarantine units in border medical quarantine work.\n4. State competent agencies shall be responsible for coordinating with the relevant agencies of other countries and with international organisations in the prevention and control of infectious diseases in border areas.\n5. The Government shall make specific provisions on border medical quarantine.\n·71· The Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam Chapter IV FIGHT AGAINST EPIDEMICS Section 1 ANNOUNCEMENT OF AN EPIDEMIC"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 37"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 38,
        "text": {
          "en": "Principles, competence, timing and conditions for the announcement of an epidemic\n1. The principles for the announcement of an epidemic are as follows:\n(1) every epidemic must be announced;\n(2) the announcement of an epidemic and of the end of an epidemic must be open, accurate and timely and must be made within the competent authority.\n2. The competence to announce an epidemic is provided for as follows:\n(1) the Chairman of the provincial People's Committee shall, on the proposal of the Director of the provincial Health Service, announce an epidemic of a Group B or Group C infectious disease;\n(2) where an epidemic has been announced in two or more provinces or centrally-run cities, the Minister of Health shall, on the proposal of the Chairman of the provincial People's Committee, announce an epidemic of a Group A infectious disease and of a number of Group B infectious diseases;\n(3) where an epidemic spreads across provinces and seriously affects human life and health, the Prime Minister shall, on the proposal of the Minister of Health, announce an epidemic of a Group A infectious disease.\n3. Within 24 hours from the receipt of a proposal for the announcement of an epidemic, the person competent under clause 2 of this Article shall decide on the announcement of the epidemic.\n4. The Prime Minister shall make specific provisions on the conditions for the announcement of an epidemic."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 38"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 39,
        "text": {
          "en": "Content of the announcement of an epidemic\n1. The content of the announcement of an epidemic comprises:\n(1) the name of the disease;\n(2) the time, place and scale of the occurrence of the epidemic;\n(3) the cause, route of transmission, nature and degree of danger of the epidemic;\n(4) measures for the prevention and control of the epidemic;\n(5) the medical examination and treatment establishments receiving and treating infectious disease patients.\n2. The content provided for in clause 1 of this Article must be notified in a timely manner to the relevant agencies, organisations and individuals so that measures for the fight against the epidemic may be carried out."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 39"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 40,
        "text": {
          "en": "Conditions and competence for the announcement of the end of an epidemic\n1. The conditions for the announcement of the end of an epidemic comprise:\n(1) no new case of infection has been detected within a given period of time and the other conditions applicable to each disease as prescribed by the Prime Minister have been satisfied.\n(2) the measures for the fight against the epidemic provided for in Section 3 of Chapter IV of this Law have been implemented.\n2. The person competent to announce an epidemic shall have the power to announce the end of the epidemic on the proposal of the competent agency provided for in clause 2 of Article 38 of this Law.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·72·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 40"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 41,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Publication of information on an epidemic] Mass media agencies shall be responsible for publishing information on the situation accurately, promptly and truthfully, in accordance with the content provided by State competent medical institutions, upon the announcement of an epidemic and upon the announcement of the end of an epidemic.\nSection 2 DECLARATION OF A STATE OF EMERGENCY DUE TO AN EPIDEMIC"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 41"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 42,
        "text": {
          "en": "Principles of and competence for the declaration of a state of emergency due to an epidemic\n1. The declaration of a state of emergency due to an epidemic shall be made in accordance with the following principles:\n(1) a state of emergency must be declared where an epidemic spreads rapidly over a wide area and seriously threatens human life and health and the economy and society of the country;\n(2) the declaration of a state of emergency must be open, accurate and timely and must be made within the competent authority.\n2. The Standing Committee of the National Assembly shall, on the proposal of the Prime Minister, issue a resolution declaring a state of emergency; where the Standing Committee of the National Assembly is unable to convene a meeting immediately, the President shall issue an order declaring a state of emergency."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 42"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 43,
        "text": {
          "en": "Content of the declaration of a state of emergency due to an epidemic\n1. The reasons for the declaration of the state of emergency.\n2. The territorial area under the state of emergency.\n3. The date and time of the commencement of the state of emergency.\n4. The competence to organise the implementation of the resolution or order declaring the state of emergency."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 43"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 44,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Competence to lift a state of emergency] Where an epidemic has been contained or stamped out, the Standing Committee of the National Assembly shall issue a resolution or the President shall issue an order lifting the state of emergency declared by it or him, on the proposal of the Prime Minister."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 44"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 45,
        "text": {
          "en": "Publication of a state of emergency due to an epidemic\n1. The Vietnam News Agency, the Voice of Vietnam, Vietnam Television, Nhan Dan newspaper and Quan Doi Nhan Dan newspaper shall be responsible for immediately publishing in full the resolution or order declaring a state of emergency due to an epidemic issued by the Standing Committee of the National Assembly or the President and the decision of the Prime Minister on the organisation of the implementation of such resolution or order, for promptly publishing information on the measures taken in the territorial area under the state of emergency and on the overcoming of the consequences of the epidemic, and for publishing in full the resolution or order lifting the state of emergency due to an epidemic issued by the Standing Committee of the National Assembly or the President.\nThe resolution or order declaring or lifting a state of emergency due to an epidemic issued by the Standing Committee of the National Assembly or the President must be publicly posted at the headquarters of agencies and organisations and at public units.\n2. Other mass media at the central and local levels shall be responsible for publishing information on the declaration and lifting of a state of emergency due to an epidemic and on the process of overcoming the consequences of the epidemic.\n·73· The Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam Section 3 MEASURES FOR THE FIGHT AGAINST EPIDEMICS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 45"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 46,
        "text": {
          "en": "Establishment of the steering committee for the fight against the epidemic\n1. A steering committee for the fight against the epidemic must be established immediately after an epidemic is announced.\n2. The composition of the steering committee for the fight against the epidemic is provided for as follows:\n(1) the national steering committee for the fight against the epidemic shall comprise representatives of the health, finance, information and communications, foreign affairs, national defence, public security and other relevant agencies. Depending on the territorial scope of the announcement of the epidemic and the nature of the epidemic, the Prime Minister may himself or herself act as, or designate one of the Deputy Prime Ministers or the Minister of Health to act as, the head of the steering committee. The Ministry of Health shall be the standing body of the steering committee.\n(2) the steering committees for the fight against the epidemic at the provincial, district and commune levels shall comprise representatives of the health, finance, information and communications, military, public security and other relevant agencies. The head of the steering committee for the fight against the epidemic shall be the Chairman of the People's Committee at the same level. The medical institution at the same level shall be the standing body of the steering committee.\n3. The steering committee for the fight against the epidemic shall be responsible for organising the implementation of measures for the fight against the epidemic and for overcoming the consequences of the epidemic, and for establishing mobile teams for the fight against the epidemic to carry out first aid, treatment and measures at epidemic foci directly.\n4. The Prime Minister shall make specific provisions on the competence to establish, organise and operate the steering committees for the fight against the epidemic at all levels."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 46"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 47,
        "text": {
          "en": "Declaration and reporting of an epidemic\n1. Where an epidemic occurs, the patient, or the person who detects the patient or the patient and a suspected case, must report it to the nearest medical institution within 24 hours from the detection of the epidemic.\n2. Upon detecting a case or receiving a report of a disease, a medical institution must report it to the People's Committee and the medical prophylaxis unit of the locality where the epidemic occurs, and must organise the implementation of measures for the fight against the epidemic as soon as possible.\n3. The Minister of Health shall make specific provisions on the regime for the declaration and reporting of epidemics."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 47"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 48,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Organisation of first aid and medical examination and treatment] The steering committee for the fight against the epidemic shall direct the adoption of the following measures and the organisation of first aid, examination and treatment for patients and suspected cases:\n1. To classify patients and provide initial treatment and timely first aid in accordance with the diagnosis and treatment guidance of the Ministry of Health.\n2. To mobilise means, medicines, medical equipment, hospital beds and examination and treatment establishments, to arrange for professional medical staff to be on duty 24 hours a day without interruption and to be ready at all times for resuscitation and examination and treatment in the service of the fight against the epidemic. Patients with Group A infectious diseases shall be examined and treated free of charge.\n3. Depending on the nature, extent and scale of the disease, the steering committee for the fight against the epidemic shall decide to take the following measures:\n(1) to organise the treatment units in the epidemic area to receive and resuscitate patients;\n(2) to dispatch mobile teams for the fight against the epidemic into the epidemic area to conduct testing, resuscitation and treatment at the patients' locations and to transfer patients to examination and treatment establishments;\n(3) to mobilise examination and treatment establishments to participate in first aid, examination and treatment for the fight against the epidemic;\n(4) to take other necessary measures provided for by law.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·74·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 48"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 49,
        "text": {
          "en": "Organisation of medical isolation\n1. Patients with the disease, persons suspected of being ill with the disease, pathogen carriers of the disease and persons who have been in contact with pathogens of Group A and of a number of Group B diseases must be isolated in accordance with the regulations of the Minister of Health.\n2. Forms of isolation comprise isolation at home, isolation in an examination and treatment establishment or isolation at other locations.\n3. Medical units in the epidemic area shall be responsible for organising isolation in accordance with the direction of the steering committee for the fight against the epidemic. Where the subjects provided for in clause 1 of this Article fail to comply with the isolation requirements of the medical unit, compulsory isolation measures shall be applied in accordance with the regulations of the Government."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 49"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 50,
        "text": {
          "en": "Hygiene, sterilisation and disinfection in an epidemic area\n1. Measures for hygiene, sterilisation and disinfection comprise:\n(1) hygiene of the environment, water, food and persons;\n(2) sterilisation and disinfection of areas where the pathogen of the disease is confirmed or suspected to be present;\n(3) destruction of animals, food and other disease transmission vectors.\n2. Mobile teams for the fight against the epidemic shall be responsible for taking measures for hygiene, sterilisation and disinfection in accordance with professional standards immediately upon a request being made by the committee for the fight against the epidemic.\n3. Agencies, organisations and individuals shall be responsible for implementing measures for hygiene, sterilisation and disinfection in accordance with the guidance of competent medical units; where such measures are not implemented voluntarily, the medical unit shall have the power to apply compulsory measures for hygiene, sterilisation and disinfection."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 50"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 51,
        "text": {
          "en": "Personal protection measures\n1. Persons participating in the fight against the epidemic and persons at risk of infection must protect themselves by taking one or several of the following measures:\n(1) use of personal protective equipment;\n(2) use of medicines for disease prevention;\n(3) use of vaccines and medical biological products for disease prevention;\n(4) use of sterilising chemicals and chemicals against disease transmission vectors.\n2. The State shall ensure the adoption of the personal protection measures provided for in clause 1 of this Article by persons participating in the fight against the epidemic."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 51"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 52,
        "text": {
          "en": "Other measures for the fight against the epidemic during an epidemic\n1. Where necessary, State competent agencies may take the following measures for the fight against the epidemic:\n(1) to suspend the operation of public catering service establishments in the epidemic area that involve a risk of transmission of the disease;\n(2) to prohibit the trading in and use of food confirmed by a competent medical unit to be a transmission vector of the disease;\n(3) to restrict the gathering of people or to suspend the operation and services of public units in the epidemic area.\n2. The Government shall make specific provisions on the taking of the measures provided for in clause 1 of this Article.\n·75· The Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 52"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 53,
        "text": {
          "en": "Control of entry into and exit from an epidemic area of a Group A disease\n1. Measures for the control of entry into and exit from an epidemic area of a Group A disease comprise:\n(1) restricting the entry of persons and means into and their exit from the epidemic area, and, where necessary, carrying out inspection, surveillance and medical treatment;\n(2) prohibiting the movement out of the epidemic area of objects, animals, plants, food and other goods capable of transmitting the disease;\n(3) applying the personal protection measures provided for in clause 1 of Article 51 of this Law to persons entering the epidemic area;\n(4) other necessary measures provided for by law.\n2. The steering committee for the fight against the epidemic shall set up quarantine checkpoints and quarantine stations at traffic hubs for entry into and exit from the epidemic area in order to implement the measures provided for in clause 1 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 53"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 54,
        "text": {
          "en": "Measures to be taken in a state of emergency due to an epidemic\n1. In a state of emergency, a steering committee for the fight against the epidemic shall be established in accordance with the provisions of point (1) of clause 2 of Article 46 of this Law.\n2. In the event of the declaration of a state of emergency due to an epidemic, the steering committee shall have the power to:\n(1) mobilise and requisition the resources provided for in this"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 54"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 55,
        "text": {
          "en": "Law;\n(2) set up signboards and guard posts and provide guidance on travel so as to avoid the epidemic area;\n(3) require means of transport to undergo inspection and medical treatment before leaving the epidemic area;\n(4) prohibit the gathering of people and other activities involving a risk of transmission of the disease in the epidemic area;\n(5) prohibit persons and means not on duty from entering the epidemic area;\n(6) organise disinfection and sterilisation on a large scale;\n(7) destroy animals, food and other objects involving a risk of transmitting the disease to humans;\n(8) take the other measures provided for in Section 3 of this Chapter.\nArticle 55 Mobilisation of resources for activities in the fight against the epidemic\n1. Depending on the nature and degree of danger of the disease and the scale of the threat to the health of the people, a person with competence may mobilise personnel and mobilise material facilities, medical equipment, medicines, chemicals, medical supplies, public service facilities, means of transport and other resources for the fight against the epidemic. Means of transport participating in the fight against the epidemic shall be given priority in accordance with the law on traffic.\n2. Property that is mobilised and returned must be cleaned, sterilised and disinfected before being returned.\n3. The Government and People's Committees at all levels shall be responsible for ensuring the conditions for the implementation of measures for the fight against the epidemic in accordance with this Law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 55"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 56,
        "text": {
          "en": "International cooperation in the fight against the epidemic\n1. Where an epidemic occurs, the Minister of Health shall, depending on the nature and degree of danger of the disease, decide on the conduct of international exchange and cooperation in disease specimens, epidemic information, professional matters, technology, equipment and funding in activities for the fight against the epidemic.\n2. In the event of the declaration of a state of emergency due to an epidemic, the Prime Minister shall call upon countries and international organisations to provide assistance in resources for the fight against the epidemic and to cooperate in taking measures to prevent the spread of the epidemic.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 3 ·76· Chapter V CONDITIONS FOR ENSURING THE PREVENTION AND CONTROL OF INFECTIOUS DISEASES"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 56"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 57,
        "text": {
          "en": "Units for the prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. Units for the prevention and control of infectious diseases comprise:\n(1) medical prophylaxis units;\n(2) medical examination and treatment establishments for infectious diseases, comprising specialised hospitals for infectious diseases, the infectious disease departments of general hospitals at the district, county, district-level town and provincial city level and above, and other medical establishments assigned the task of examining and treating infectious diseases.\n2. General hospitals at the district, county, district-level town and provincial city level and above must have an infectious disease department.\n3. The Minister of Health shall make provisions on the location, design and material facility conditions as well as the technology, equipment and human resources of medical examination and treatment establishments for infectious diseases."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 57"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 58,
        "text": {
          "en": "Training and fostering of staff for the prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. The State shall make plans for and give priority to the professional training, retraining and fostering of staff for the prevention and control of infectious diseases.\n2. The Minister of Health shall preside over and coordinate with the Minister of Education and Training and the heads of the relevant agencies and organisations in organising the professional training, retraining and fostering of staff for the prevention and control of infectious diseases."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 58"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 59,
        "text": {
          "en": "Regimes applicable to staff for the prevention and control of infectious diseases and to persons participating in the fight against the epidemic\n1. Staff for the prevention and control of infectious diseases may enjoy the occupational allowance regime and other preferential regimes.\n2. Persons participating in the fight against the epidemic may enjoy the allowance regime for the fight against the epidemic and, where they are infected with the disease, the occupational risk regime.\n3. Where a person participating in the fight against the epidemic dies or is injured in the course of the fight against the epidemic as a result of heroic action to save others, consideration shall be given to recognising him or her as a martyr or a wounded soldier, and he or she shall enjoy policies equivalent to those applicable to wounded soldiers in accordance with the law on preferential treatment of persons with meritorious services to the revolution.\n4. The Prime Minister shall make specific provisions on the regimes provided for in clauses 1, 2 and 3 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 59"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 60,
        "text": {
          "en": "Funding for the prevention and control of infectious diseases\n1. Funding for the prevention and control of infectious diseases comprises:\n(1) the State budget;\n(2) aid funds;\n(3) other sources of funding provided for by law.\n2. The State shall ensure an adequate and timely budget for activities for the prevention and control of infectious diseases every year, and the budget for the prevention and control of infectious diseases may not be used for other purposes."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 60"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 61,
        "text": {
          "en": "State reserves for the prevention and control of epidemics\n1. The State shall maintain State reserves of funding, medicines, chemicals and medical equipment for the prevention and control of epidemics.\n2. The establishment, organisation, management, allocation and use of State reserves for the prevention and control of epidemics must comply with the law on State reserves.\n·77· The Law on Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 61"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 62,
        "text": {
          "en": "Support fund for the prevention and control of epidemics\n1. The support fund for the prevention and control of epidemics shall be established and shall operate in accordance with law for the treatment and nursing of infectious disease patients and for other activities for the prevention and control of epidemics.\n2. The financial resources of the fund shall come from voluntary contributions and sponsorship by domestic and foreign organisations and individuals.\nChapter VI IMPLEMENTATION PROVISIONS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 62"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 63,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Effect] This Law shall take effect from 1 July 2008."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 63"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 64,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Guidance for implementation] The Government shall make specific provisions on this Law and shall guide its implementation."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 64"
        }
      }
    ]
  };

