  TR["VN-H076"] = {
    "lawId": "VN-H076",
    "sourceLang": "zh",
    "targetLang": "en",
    "engine": "llm",
    "model": "deepseek-v4.1-flash",
    "promptVersion": "calr-vi2en-v1",
    "translatedAt": "2026-10-28",
    "reviewed": "machine",
    "completenessNote": "逐条对译，共 30 条；条号与原文一一对应，未合并、未省略。",
    "articles": [
      {
        "no": 1,
        "text": {
          "en": "Law on Value Added Tax (No. 02/1997/QH9 of 10 May 1997) In order to promote production, expand the circulation of goods and services, encourage the development of the national economy and mobilise part of the income of consumers for the State budget;\nPursuant to the 1992 Constitution of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam;\nthis Law provides for value added tax.\nChapter I GENERAL PROVISIONS\n[Value added tax] Value added tax is a tax calculated on the added value of goods and services arising in the course of production, circulation and consumption."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 1"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 2,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Dutiable objects] Goods and services used in Vietnam for production, business and consumption are dutiable objects, except those provided for in Article 4\nof this Law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 2"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 3,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Taxpayers] Units and individuals producing and trading in dutiable goods and services (collectively referred to as business units) and other units and individuals importing dutiable goods (collectively referred to as importers) are subject to value added tax."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 3"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 4,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Objects not subject to value added tax] The following goods and services are not subject to value added tax:\n1. Products of cultivation, husbandry and aquaculture which have not been processed into other products or have undergone only ordinary processing by units or individuals producing and selling their own products;\n2. Salt;\n3. Goods and services subject to special sales tax shall not be subject to value added tax at the stage at which special sales tax is paid;\n4. Specialised equipment, machinery and means of transport which cannot yet be produced domestically and which units import as fixed assets under investment projects;\n5. Transfer of land use rights within the scope of land use rights subject to transfer tax;\n6. Dwelling houses owned by the State and sold by the State to tenants;\n7. Credit and investment fund services;\n8. Life insurance; student insurance; insurance of poultry and crops and insurance of various kinds for non-business purposes;\n9. Medical services;\n10. Cultural, exhibition and sports activities, art performances and film production for non-business purposes;\n11. The issuance and projection of celluloid films and video films made from materials;\n12. Teaching and the provision of training;\n13. Printing, publication and distribution of newspapers, magazines, professional journals, political books, textbooks, curricula, legal documents and books in the languages of ethnic minorities; pictures, photographs and posters for propaganda purposes; banknotes;\n14. Public services for road and residential area sanitation and waste removal; maintenance of zoos, gardens, parks, road greenery and public lighting; funeral services;\n15. The repair, restoration and construction of cultural and artistic works, public service projects, infrastructure and houses of gratitude using funds donated by the people and funds of humanitarian aid;\n16. Public buses carrying passengers;\n17. Geological survey and prospecting, and the surveying and mapping of maps belonging to the category of basic State surveys;\n18. Irrigation and drainage serving agricultural production; domestic water for rural, mountainous, island and remote areas exploited by units and individuals on their own initiative;\n19. Weapons and equipment for the protection of national defence and security;\n20. Imported goods in the following cases: goods constituting humanitarian aid or non-refundable aid; gifts to State agencies, political organisations, socio-political organisations, social organisations, socio-professional organisations and people's armed units; articles of foreign organisations and individuals enjoying diplomatic immunity; articles carried in person within the duty-free baggage allowance;\n21. Goods re-exported, in transit or transported through Vietnam; goods temporarily imported for re-export and goods temporarily exported for re-import;\n22. Goods and services supplied directly to international transport and to Vietnamese consumers abroad;\n23. Technology transfer;\n24. Imported gold in bars or blocks which has not been processed into fine art articles, ornaments or other products;\n25. Cases where the export product is a mineral product not yet processed after extraction, to be specifically provided for by the Government;\n26. Goods and services of business households with low income. The low income threshold shall be provided for by the Government.\nGoods and services not subject to value added tax as provided for in this Article may not be credited or refunded in respect of input tax.\nWhere necessary, the Standing Committee of the National Assembly may amend and supplement the list of goods and services not subject to value added tax provided for in this Article and shall report so that the National Assembly may ratify it at its nearest session."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 4"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 5,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Obligations and responsibilities for the implementation of the Law on Value Added Tax]\n1. Business units and importers are obliged to pay tax in full and on time in accordance with this Law.\n2. The tax authorities are responsible, within the scope of their tasks and powers, for correctly implementing this Law.\n3. State agencies, political organisations, socio-political organisations, social organisations, socio-professional organisations and people's armed units shall, within the scope of their duties, tasks and powers, supervise and coordinate with the tax authorities in the implementation of the Law on Value Added Tax.\n4. Vietnamese citizens are responsible for assisting the tax authorities and tax officials in the implementation of this Law.\nChapter II BASES AND METHODS OF TAX CALCULATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 5"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 6,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Bases for tax calculation] The bases for calculating value added tax are the tax calculation price and the tax rate."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 6"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 7,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Tax calculation price] The value added tax calculation price is provided for as follows:\n1. For goods and services, it is the selling price exclusive of value added tax;\n2. For imported goods, it is the import price at the port plus import duty;\n3. For goods and services used for exchange or internal use or given as gifts, it is the value added tax calculation price of like or similar goods and services at the time the activity takes place;\n4. For activities of leasing property, it is the rent collected in instalments;\n5. For goods sold on a deferred payment basis, it is the selling price of the goods calculated on a lump-sum payment basis, not the amount paid in instalments;\n6. For processed goods, it is the processing price;\n7. For other goods and services, it is the price provided for by the Government;\n8. The value added tax calculation price of the goods and services provided for in this Article includes the service charges and surcharges enjoyed by the business establishment.\nWhere a taxpayer has turnover in foreign currency, it shall be converted into Vietnam dong at the official exchange rate published by the State Bank of Vietnam at the time the turnover arises in order to determine the tax calculation price."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 7"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 8,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Tax rates] The value added tax rates are provided for as follows:\n1. Exported goods: the rate is 0 per cent;\n2. Goods and services subject to the rate of 5 per cent:\n(a) Water for production and domestic use;\n(b) Fertilisers and ores for the production of fertilisers; insecticides and production agents promoting the production of poultry and crops;\n(c) Medical equipment and instruments; medical cotton and bandages;\n(d) Medicines for the treatment and prevention of diseases;\n(dd) Teaching and learning aids;\n(e) Toys; scientific and technical books, literary and artistic books, children's books and legal documents, except those provided for in item 13 of Article 4 of this Law;\n(g) Unprocessed products of cultivation, husbandry and aquaculture, including breeding stock, seedlings and seeds, except those provided for in item 1 of Article 4 of this Law;\n(h) Unprocessed forestry products (except timber and bamboo shoots) and fresh foodstuffs;\n(i) Domestic raw cotton;\n(k) Feed for livestock and poultry and feed for other animals and poultry;\n(l) Science and technology services;\n(m) Services directly serving agricultural production;\n3. Goods and services subject to the rate of 10 per cent:\n(a) Petroleum, natural gas, raw coal, ores and other minerals;\n(b) Electricity;\n(c) Electronic, mechanical and electrical products;\n(d) Chemical raw materials and handicraft articles;\n(dd) Fibres and fabrics, and sewing and embroidery products;\n(e) Paper and paper products;\n(g) Sugar, milk, cakes, confectionery, beverages and other kinds of processed foodstuffs;\n(h) Ceramic, porcelain, glass, rubber and plastic articles, timber and timber products; cement, bricks, tiles and other construction materials;\n(i) Construction and installation;\n(k) Transport and loading and unloading;\n(l) Postal, post and telecommunications services;\n(m) Leasing of houses, warehouses, wharves, factories, machinery, equipment and means of transport;\n(n) Legal consultancy services;\n(o) Photographing, printing and enlarging of photographs; tape recording, tape transcription and tape leasing;\nvideo recording, video projection and photocopying;\n(p) Hair perming, tailoring, hair dyeing, laundering and ironing, and bleaching;\n(q) Other kinds of goods and services not provided for in paragraphs 1, 2 and 4 of this Article, except goods subject to special sales tax at the import and export stage;\n4. Goods and services subject to the rate of 20 per cent:\n(a) Gold, silver and precious stones purchased and sold by business establishments;\n(b) Hotels, tourism and catering;\n(c) Construction lotteries and other kinds of lottery;\n(d) Shipping agents;\n(dd) Media services;\nWhere necessary, the Standing Committee of the National Assembly may amend and supplement the list of goods and services at the tax rates provided for in this Article and shall report so that the National Assembly may ratify it at its nearest session."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 8"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 9,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Methods of tax calculation] Value added tax payable shall be calculated by the tax credit method or by the method of direct calculation on the added value.\n1. The tax credit method.\nThe tax payable is equal to the output value added tax less the input value added tax.\nThe output value added tax is equal to the tax calculation price of the goods or services sold multiplied by the tax rate.\nThe input value added tax is equal to the total value added tax already settled and recorded on the value added invoices for the purchase of goods and services or on the documents evidencing payment of value added tax on imported goods.\n2. The method of direct calculation on the added value.\nThe tax payable is equal to the added value of the goods or services multiplied by the value added tax rate.\nThe added value is equal to the settled price of the goods or services sold less the settled price of the corresponding goods or services purchased.\nThe method of direct calculation on the added value shall apply only to the following objects:\n(a) Business households and foreign organisations and individuals doing business in Vietnam which do not comply with the Law on Foreign Investment in Vietnam and do not satisfy the conditions as to accounting, invoices and documents for the tax credit method to serve as the basis for tax calculation.\n(b) Business establishments purchasing and selling gold, silver and precious stones."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 9"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 10,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Credit of input value added tax]\n1. Business establishments paying value added tax by the tax credit method may credit the following input value added tax (referred to as input tax):\n(a) The input tax on goods and services used for the production and business of goods and services subject to value added tax may be credited in full;\n(b) The input tax on goods and services used simultaneously for the production and business of goods and services subject to and not subject to value added tax may be credited to the extent that the goods and services are used for the production and business of goods and services subject to value added tax;\n(c) The input tax arising in a month may be declared and credited in determining the tax payable for that month; however, for fixed assets, where the input tax to be credited is large, it may be credited gradually or refunded in accordance with regulations of the Government;\n(d) For producers and processors of unprocessed agricultural, forestry and aquacultural products purchased from producers without value added invoices, input tax equal to 1 per cent to 5 per cent of the purchase price of the agricultural, forestry and aquacultural products may be credited; the crediting rate for each kind of goods shall be provided for by the Government;\nthe tax credit provided for in this point shall not apply to exports;\n(dd) In other special cases, the crediting of input tax shall be provided for by the Government.\n2. The bases for determining the amount of input tax creditable are:\n(a) The amount of value added tax recorded on the value added invoices for the purchase of goods and services or on the documents evidencing payment of value added tax on imported goods; where the goods and services purchased have no value added invoice or the invoice does not record the amount of value added tax, no tax credit may be made;\n(b) The lists of goods and services purchased provided for in paragraph 1 of this Article, in the form prescribed by the tax authority."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 10"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 11,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Invoices and documents]\n1. The purchase and sale of goods must, in accordance with law, be accompanied by invoices and documents.\n2. Business establishments paying tax by the tax credit method shall use value added invoices and shall record fully and accurately the prescribed elements, namely the amount paid, surcharges, value added tax and the settled price.\nWhere an invoice does not record the value added tax, the value added tax shall be determined as the settled price on the invoice multiplied by the value added tax rate.\n3. For various kinds of stamps and tickets on which the settled price has already been printed and which may be regarded as settlement documents, the settled price on the stamps and tickets shall be deemed to include value added tax.\nChapter III REGISTRATION, DECLARATION, TAX PAYMENT AND TAX SETTLEMENT"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 11"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 12,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Tax registration]\n1. Business establishments, including their subordinate units, shall register for value added tax payment with the tax authority directly managing them in accordance with the instructions of the tax authority. For newly established business establishments, the time limit for registration for value added tax payment shall not exceed ten days from the date on which the business registration certificate is issued. In the case of merger, consolidation, separation, division, dissolution, bankruptcy or change of business, the business establishment shall report to the tax authority fifteen days before the merger, consolidation, separation, division, dissolution, bankruptcy or change of business.\n2. Where a business establishment paying tax by the method of direct calculation on the added value fully complies with the rules on documents, invoices and accounting books and voluntarily registers to pay tax by the tax credit method, the tax authority shall examine and process the registration."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 12"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 13,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Tax declaration]\n1. Business establishments shall declare value added tax monthly and shall submit the tax registration form to the tax authority within the first ten days of the following month. Where no input tax arises, the business establishment shall still declare and submit the registration form to the tax authority. Business establishments shall complete the form in accordance with the correct tax registration format and shall be responsible for the accuracy of their declarations.\n2. Business establishments and importers of goods shall, on each occasion of importation of goods, declare and submit the value added tax registration form and at the same time declare import duty to the tax authority of the locality of the import port.\n3. Temporary business establishments shall, on each transaction and before transporting the goods, declare and pay tax to the tax authority of the locality where the goods are purchased.\n4. Establishments concurrently trading in several kinds of goods and services subject to different value added tax rates shall declare value added tax at the rate applicable to each kind of goods and services. Where a business establishment is unable to determine the tax by each rate, it shall calculate and pay tax at the highest rate applicable to the goods and services it produces and trades in.\nThe Ministry of Finance shall prescribe the form of the tax registration form and provide guidance on declaration matters."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 13"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 14,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Value added tax shall be paid into the State budget as follows:]\n1. Business establishments shall pay value added tax in full and on time in accordance with the tax notice of the tax authority.\nThe time limit for payment of tax for a month shall be recorded in the notice and shall not be later than the twenty-fifth day of the following month.\n2. Business establishments and importers shall pay value added tax on each occasion of importation. The time limit for notification and payment of value added tax on imported goods shall be determined in accordance with the time limit for notification and payment of import duty.\n3. In the period of tax settlement, where a business establishment paying value added tax by the tax credit method has input tax greater than output tax, the excess shall be credited in the next period of tax settlement.\nWhere a business establishment has newly invested in fixed assets and the input tax to be credited is large, the provisions of point (c) of paragraph 1 of Article 10 of this Law shall apply.\n4. Value added tax shall be paid into the State budget in Vietnam dong."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 14"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 15,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Tax settlement] Business establishments shall settle tax with the tax authority annually. The tax settlement year shall be the calendar year.\nWithin sixty days from the end of the year, business establishments shall submit the settlement report to the tax authority and, within ten days from the submission of the settlement report, shall pay in full the outstanding tax to the State budget; where an overpayment is made, it shall be credited against the tax payable in the next period.\nIn the case of merger, consolidation, separation, division, dissolution or bankruptcy, business establishments shall complete tax settlement with the tax authority and shall submit the settlement report to the tax authority within forty-five days from the date of the decision on the merger, consolidation, separation, division, dissolution or bankruptcy."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 15"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 16,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Tax refund The cases in which value added tax is refunded are as follows:]\n1. Where a business establishment paying tax by the tax credit method has, in a quarter, input tax credited in the months of the quarter greater than output tax, the tax may be refunded quarterly, or the input tax on fixed assets may be refunded in accordance with point (c) of paragraph 1 of Article 10 of this Law;\n2. Where tax is settled and paid in excess upon merger, consolidation, separation, division, dissolution or bankruptcy;\n3. Where the competent authority issues a decision on refund of tax in accordance with law.\nThe Ministry of Finance shall, on the basis of this Article, provide specifically for the procedures and competence for settling tax refunds."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 16"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 17,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Tasks, powers and responsibilities of the tax authorities] The tax authorities have the following tasks, powers and responsibilities:\n1. To guide business establishments registering for business to comply with the regime of registration, declaration and tax payment in accordance with this Law;\n2. To notify business establishments correctly and in accordance with regulations of the tax payable and the time limit for payment; where the time limit for payment recorded in the notice has passed and the business establishment has not yet paid, to continue to notify the tax payable and the late payment charge in accordance with paragraph 2 of Article 19 of this Law; where the business establishment still fails to pay the tax and penalty in full, to apply the measures provided for in paragraph 4 of Article 19 of this Law in order to ensure collection of the tax and penalty in full; where those measures have been applied and the business establishment still fails to pay the tax and penalty in full, to transfer the file to the State agency with competence to decide for handling in accordance with law;\n3. To inspect and supervise the declaration, payment and settlement of tax by business establishments in order to ensure correct compliance with the law;\n4. To handle administrative violations in the field of taxation and to receive complaints relating to taxation;\n5. To require taxpayers to provide accounting books, invoices, documents and other files and materials relating to tax calculation and tax payment; to require credit institutions, banks and other units and individuals concerned to provide files and materials relating to tax calculation and tax payment;\n6. To preserve and use, in accordance with regulations, the data and materials provided by business establishments and other objects."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 17"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 18,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Power to determine tax]\n1. In the following cases, the tax authority shall determine the tax payable by the taxpayer:\n(a) Failure to comply with, or incorrect compliance with, the regime of accounting, invoices and documents;\n(b) Failure to declare, or incorrect compliance after the time limit for submission of the registration form notified; or submission of the tax registration form where the declaration does not conform to the bases for determining the value added tax amount;\n(c) Refusal to produce accounting books, invoices, documents and materials relating to the calculation of value added tax;\n(d) Discovery of business conducted without business registration.\n2. The tax authority shall determine the tax payable on the basis of the materials of investigation into the business activities of the business establishment or on the basis of the tax payable by business establishments in the same trade having a comparable scale of business.\nWhere it does not agree with the tax payable determined, the business establishment has the right to complain to the tax authority at the immediately higher level; while awaiting settlement, the business establishment must still pay tax in accordance with the tax amount determined.\nChapter IV PENALTIES AND COMMENDATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 18"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 19,
        "text": {
          "en": "Handling of [violations of the tax law by taxpayers] Where a taxpayer violates the Law on Value Added Tax, it shall be dealt with as follows:\n1. Failure to comply correctly with the provisions on registration, declaration, tax payment and tax settlement and with the regime of accounting and retention of invoices and documents provided for in Articles\n11, 12, 13, 14 and 15 of this Law shall, depending on the nature and extent of the violation, be subject to an administrative penalty for a violation in the field of taxation;\n2. Where tax or a penalty is paid later than the time limit for payment or the time limit in the decision on tax handling, the taxpayer shall, in addition to paying the tax and penalty in full, pay a late payment charge of 0.1 per cent (one thousandth) for each day of delay;\n3. Concealment or evasion of tax shall, in addition to payment of the tax in full in accordance with law, be subject, depending on the nature and extent of the violation, to a fine of one to five times the amount of tax fraudulently withheld; where the amount of tax evaded is large or an administrative penalty for a violation in the field of taxation has already been imposed in full but the violation continues or other serious violations are committed, criminal liability shall be pursued in accordance with law;\n4. Where tax or a penalty is not paid in accordance with the notice or the decision on tax handling, the following measures shall be taken:\n(a) Deduction from the deposits of the taxpayer at banks, the State Treasury or credit organisations in order to pay the tax and penalty;\nbefore collecting the debt, banks, the State Treasury and credit organisations are responsible for deducting funds from the deposit accounts of the taxpayer in order to pay the tax and penalty into the State budget in accordance with the decision on tax handling of the tax authority or the competent authority.\n(b) Retention of goods in order to ensure the collection of the tax and penalty in full.\n(c) Registration of property in accordance with law in order to ensure the collection of the outstanding tax and penalty in full."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 19"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 20,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Powers of the tax authorities in handling violations of the tax law]\n1. The head of the tax authority directly managing the tax has the power to handle the violations of taxpayers provided for in paragraphs 1, 2 and 3 of Article 19 of this Law;\n2. For the violations provided for in paragraph 3 of Article 19 of this Law, the director or the head of the division of the tax authority directly managing the tax may apply the measures provided for in paragraph 4 of Article 19 of this Law and transfer the file to the competent authority for handling in accordance with law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 20"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 21,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Handling of violations by tax officials and other individuals]\n1. Tax officials and other individuals who take advantage of their position or powers to use or appropriate unlawfully tax or a penalty shall return in full the tax or penalty so used or appropriated and shall, depending on the nature and extent of the violation, be subject to disciplinary action or criminal liability in accordance with law.\n2. Tax officials and other individuals who, through lack of responsibility or through erroneous handling, cause loss to taxpayers shall compensate for the loss in accordance with law and shall, depending on the nature and extent of the violation, be subject to disciplinary action or criminal liability in accordance with law.\n3. Tax officials and other individuals who take advantage of their position or powers to collude with or shield persons violating the Law on Value Added Tax or committing other acts in violation of this Law shall, depending on the nature and extent of the violation, be subject to disciplinary action or criminal liability in accordance with law.\n4. Persons who obstruct or instigate others to obstruct the implementation of the Law on Value Added Tax shall, depending on the nature and extent of the violation, be subject to an administrative penalty or criminal liability in accordance with law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 21"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 22,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Commendation] Commendation shall be given to tax authorities and tax officials that accomplish outstandingly the tasks assigned to them; to organisations and individuals that achieve results in the implementation of the Law on Value Added Tax; and to taxpayers that perform well their duty to pay tax.\nThe Government shall provide specifically for the forms of commendation.\nChapter V COMPLAINTS AND PRESCRIPTION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 22"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 23,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Powers and responsibilities of taxpayers in tax complaints]\n1. A taxpayer has the right to complain about cases in which a tax official or tax authority enforces the law in a manner inconsistent with the Law on Value Added Tax.\nThe complaint shall be sent to the tax authority directly managing the tax within thirty days from the date of receipt of the notice or the decision on handling of the tax official or tax authority.\nWhile awaiting settlement, the taxpayer shall still comply with the notice or decision on handling of the tax authority.\n2. Where the complainant does not agree with the decision of the agency receiving the complaint, or where the matter has not been settled after the time limit provided for in this Law"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 23"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 24,
        "text": {
          "en": "has expired, the complainant has the right to appeal to the tax authority at the immediately higher level or to appeal to a court in accordance with law.\nArticle 24 [Responsibilities and powers of the tax authorities in receiving tax complaints]\n1. Upon receipt of a tax complaint, the tax authority shall examine and settle it within fifteen days from the date of receipt of the complaint; for complicated cases the time limit may be extended appropriately but shall not exceed thirty days; where the case is outside its competence, it shall transfer the file or report to the competent authority for handling within ten days from the date of receipt of the complaint and shall notify the complainant.\n2. The agency receiving the complaint has the power to require the complainant to provide files and materials in support of the complaint; where the complainant refuses to provide files and materials, it has the power to refuse to examine and receive the complaint.\n3. The tax authority shall return unreasonable tax or penalties to the taxpayer within ten days from the date of receipt of the decision of the higher-level authority or of the authority with competence to decide in accordance with law.\n4. Where fraud or tax evasion is discovered and confirmed, the tax authority is responsible for recovering the tax and penalty or refunding the tax in respect of the preceding five years from the date on which the inspection discovers the fraud, tax evasion or error in tax; where a business establishment fails to register, declare and pay tax, the time limit for recovery of the tax and penalty shall run from the date on which the business establishment commenced business.\n5. The head of the higher-level tax authority is responsible for settling the tax complaint of a taxpayer against the lower-level tax authority.\nThe decision of the Minister of Finance on a tax complaint shall be final.\nChapter VI ORGANISATION OF IMPLEMENTATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 24"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 25,
        "text": {
          "en": "The Government shall direct the organisation of the implementation of the Law on Value Added Tax throughout the country."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 25"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 26,
        "text": {
          "en": "The Minister of Finance shall be responsible for organising the implementation and inspecting the implementation of the Law on Value Added Tax throughout the country."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 26"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 27,
        "text": {
          "en": "People's Committees at all levels shall, within the scope of their tasks and powers, direct and inspect the implementation of the Law on Value Added Tax in their localities.\nChapter VII IMPLEMENTATION"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 27"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 28,
        "text": {
          "en": "The Law on Value Added Tax shall take effect from 1 January 1999.\nThe Law on Turnover Tax and the Law Amending and Supplementing a Number of Articles of the Law on Turnover Tax and of Other Legal Documents on Turnover Tax shall be repealed from the date on which the Law on Value Added Tax takes effect.\nMatters outstanding before 1 January 1999 concerning tax, tax settlement and tax exemption or reduction, and the handling of violations of the law on turnover tax, shall be dealt with in accordance with the corresponding provisions of the Law on Turnover Tax and the Law Amending and Supplementing a Number of Articles of the Law on Turnover Tax and of Other Legal Documents on Turnover Tax.\nWhere, in the first years of application of the Law on Value Added Tax, production, construction and transport units suffer losses because the tax payable is greater than the tax calculated under the former Law on Turnover Tax, the value added tax payable shall be reduced. The reduction shall correspond to the loss caused on that ground but shall not exceed 50 per cent of the tax payable. The reduction shall be reviewed annually and settled by calendar year but shall not exceed three years from the date on which the Law on Value Added Tax takes effect.\nThe Government shall, in accordance with this Article, provide specifically for the review and reduction of value added tax."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 28"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 29,
        "text": {
          "en": "Where an international treaty concluded or acceded to by Vietnam contains provisions on import duty and export duty that differ from this Law, value added tax shall be governed by the international treaty."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 29"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 30,
        "text": {
          "en": "The Government shall provide for the detailed implementation of this Law.\nThis Law was adopted on 10 May 1997 at the Eleventh Session of the Ninth National Assembly of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam.\nChairman of the National Assembly Nong Duc Manh"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 30"
        }
      }
    ]
  };

  TR["VN-H077"] = {
    "lawId": "VN-H077",
    "sourceLang": "zh",
    "targetLang": "en",
    "engine": "llm",
    "model": "deepseek-v4.1-flash",
    "promptVersion": "calr-vi2en-v1",
    "translatedAt": "2026-10-28",
    "reviewed": "machine",
    "completenessNote": "逐条对译，共 41 条；条号与原文一一对应，未合并、未省略。",
    "articles": [
      {
        "no": 1,
        "text": {
          "en": "·97· The Law on the Vietnam Coast Guard of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam Chapter I GENERAL PROVISIONS\n[Scope of regulation] This Law provides for the position, functions, tasks, powers and principles of organisation and operation of the Vietnam Coast Guard; the regimes and policies applicable to the Vietnam Coast Guard; and the responsibilities of the agencies, organisations and individuals concerned."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 1"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 2,
        "text": {
          "en": "Interpretation of terms In this Law, the following terms shall be construed as follows:\n1. Safeguarding the sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction of the State in the Vietnamese sea area means the acts of preventing, detecting and handling violations of the laws and regulations of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam concerning its sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction committed by agencies, organisations or individuals.\n2. Officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard comprise non-commissioned officers, professional soldiers, corporals, privates, workers and personnel on the payroll of the Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 2"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 3,
        "text": {
          "en": "Position and functions of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. The Vietnam Coast Guard is a people's armed force, a specialised force of the State and the core force for law enforcement in safeguarding peace, order and security at sea of the country.\n2. The Vietnam Coast Guard shall be responsible for advising the Minister of National Defence on the promulgation, within his competence, and on the formulation by the Party and the State of policies and laws on the protection of maritime security, order and peace of the country; for safeguarding the sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction of the State in the Vietnamese sea area; and for managing security, order and safety within its competence and ensuring the implementation of Vietnamese law, international treaties and international agreements to which the Socialist Republic of Vietnam is a party."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 3"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 4,
        "text": {
          "en": "Principles of the organisation and operation of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. Absolute and direct submission to the leadership of the Communist Party of Vietnam, the overall command of the President, the unified management of the Government and the direct direction and command of the Minister of National Defence.\n2. Compliance with the Constitution and laws of Vietnam and the international treaties to which the Socialist Republic of Vietnam is a party.\n3. Centralised and unified management from the Command of the Vietnam Coast Guard down to grassroots units.\n* Translator: Wang Jiawei, research intern at the National Marine Information Centre.\n* Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 1 ·98·\n4. Proactive defence, detection, struggle against and handling of unlawful acts.\n5. Management of maritime peace, order and security in conjunction with the task of safeguarding the sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction of the State and with the development of the marine economy.\n6. Reliance on the people, giving play to the strength of the people and submitting to the supervision of the people."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 4"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 5,
        "text": {
          "en": "Building of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. The State shall build a revolutionary, regular, elite and modern Vietnam Coast Guard and shall allocate priority resources for its development.\n2. Vietnamese agencies, organisations and workers shall be responsible for participating in building a clean and strong Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 5"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 6,
        "text": {
          "en": "Responsibilities, regimes and policies of agencies, organisations and individuals in participating in, coordinating with and assisting the work of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. Agencies, organisations and individuals carrying out activities in the Vietnamese sea area shall be responsible for participating in, coordinating with and assisting the work of the Vietnam Coast Guard so that it may perform its functions, tasks and powers.\n2. Vietnamese agencies, organisations and citizens shall be responsible for coordinating with the Vietnam Coast Guard in implementing the decisions on the mobilisation of civilian manpower, vessels, tools and technical equipment issued by the competent authorities for the safeguarding of the sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction of the State in the Vietnamese sea area.\n3. Where agencies, organisations and individuals participate in, coordinate with or assist the work of the Coast Guard, the State shall keep confidential information relating to them at their request;\nthose with meritorious achievements shall be commended, those suffering loss of property shall be compensated, those whose reputation is damaged shall be assisted in restoring it, and where they are injured or their life or health is harmed, their relatives or families shall enjoy the regimes and policies provided for by law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 6"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 7,
        "text": {
          "en": "Strictly prohibited acts\n1. Opposing or obstructing the activities of the Vietnam Coast Guard; retaliating against, intimidating or infringing upon the life, health, dignity or reputation of officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard while they are performing official duties or for reasons of such duties.\n2. Bribing, corrupting or coercing officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard into violating their duties, tasks and powers.\n3. Impersonating officers or soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard, or counterfeiting vessels or facilities of the Vietnam Coast Guard; forging, trading in or unlawfully using the uniforms, seals or official documents of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n4. Exploiting or abusing the posts, powers and positions of officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard to violate the law or to infringe upon the lawful rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals.\n5. Officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard extorting money or property from, or creating difficulties for the lawful activities of, agencies, organisations and individuals in the Vietnamese sea area.\n6. Other acts in violation of the provisions of this Law.\nChapter II TASKS AND POWERS OF THE VIETNAM COAST GUARD"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 7"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 8,
        "text": {
          "en": "Tasks of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. To collect, analyse, assess and forecast situations, to propose guidelines, solutions and plans for safeguarding national security and for the enforcement of the law at sea; and to conduct research, analysis, forecasting and advice for the competent authorities at all levels in the promulgation of policies and laws on safeguarding the sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction of the State in the Vietnamese sea area, ensuring order and security at sea and preventing and combating crimes and unlawful activities at sea.\n2. To safeguard the sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction of the State, the security and interests of the nation and the nation-State, to protect marine resources and the marine environment, and to protect the lawful property, rights and interests of agencies, organisations and individuals at sea.\n3. To prevent and combat crimes and unlawful acts at sea, to protect peace, order and security at sea, to conduct search and rescue at sea, and to participate in responding to environmental incidents at sea.\n4. To participate in building the national defence and security system and to handle national defence and security situations at sea.\n5. To disseminate, popularise and provide education on the law.\n6. To receive and use civilian manpower, vessels, tools and technical equipment mobilised for participation in safeguarding the sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction in the Vietnamese sea area.\n7. To carry out international cooperation on the basis of international agreements and international treaties to which the Socialist Republic of Vietnam is a signatory that relate to the functions, tasks and powers of the Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 8"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 9,
        "text": {
          "en": "Powers of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. To patrol, inspect and search persons, vessels, goods and luggage in the Vietnamese sea area in accordance with this Law and other relevant laws.\n2. To use the weapons, explosives and supporting tools required in the performance of its tasks in accordance with Article 14 of this Law.\n3. To use facilities and specialised technical equipment in accordance with Article 15 of this Law and other relevant laws.\n4. To handle cases of administrative violation in accordance with the law on the handling of administrative violations.\n5. To carry out a number of criminal investigation activities in accordance with the law on the organisation of criminal investigation agencies and the law on criminal procedure.\n6. To pursue vessels committing violations at sea.\n7. To call upon persons, vessels, tools and technical equipment belonging to Vietnamese agencies, organisations and citizens in emergency situations.\n8. To propose in emergency situations that foreign organisations and individuals carry out rescue and assistance activities in the Vietnamese sea area.\n9. To detain vessels committing violations at sea in accordance with law.\n10. To carry out its work in accordance with the provisions of Article 12 of this Law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 9"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 10,
        "text": {
          "en": "Obligations and responsibilities of officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. Absolute loyalty to the Fatherland, the people, the Party and the State; strict implementation of the guidelines and line of the Party, the policies and laws of the State and the orders and instructions of superiors.\n2. Firmly safeguarding the sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction of the State in the Vietnamese sea area; preventing and combating crimes and unlawful acts at sea, ensuring social peace, order and security and protecting peace, stability and development in the Vietnamese sea area.\n3. Strictly protecting State secrets and work secrets and strictly complying with the working regulations of the Coast Guard.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 1 ·100·\n4. Complying with the international treaties signed by Vietnam and the international agreements relating to the functions, tasks and powers of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n5. Intensifying study so as to improve political fortitude, legal awareness, professional standards, operational capacity and awareness of organisational discipline, and carrying out physical training.\n6. Being responsible before the law and before the decisions of superiors in the performance of their tasks.\nChapter III ACTIVITIES OF THE VIETNAM COAST GUARD Part 1 SCOPE OF ACTIVITIES AND WORKING METHODS OF THE VIETNAM COAST GUARD"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 10"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 11,
        "text": {
          "en": "Scope of activities\n1. The Vietnam Coast Guard shall carry out activities in the Vietnamese sea area in the performance of the functions, tasks and powers provided for in this Law.\n2. For humanitarian and peaceful purposes and in the prevention of and combat against crimes and unlawful activities, the Vietnam Coast Guard may carry out activities outside the Vietnamese sea area, and in doing so shall comply with Vietnamese law, the international treaties signed by Vietnam and the international agreements relating to the functions, tasks and powers of the Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 11"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 12,
        "text": {
          "en": "Working methods of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. In carrying out its work, the Vietnam Coast Guard shall apply the mass movement method, the legal method, the diplomatic method, the economic method, the scientific and technological method, the operational method and the armed method.\n2. The Commander of the Vietnam Coast Guard shall decide, in accordance with the provisions of clause 1 of this Article, on the methods to be applied by the Coast Guard and shall be responsible before the law and before superiors for his or her decisions.\nPart 2 LAW ENFORCEMENT AT SEA BY THE VIETNAM COAST GUARD"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 12"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 13,
        "text": {
          "en": "Patrol, inspection and search\n1. The Vietnam Coast Guard shall carry out patrol, inspection and search activities in respect of persons, vessels, goods and luggage so as to detect, prevent and handle unlawful acts at sea.\n2. The situations in which a vessel is required to stop for inspection and search comprise:\n(a) where a violation or signs of a violation are directly detected;\n(b) where a violation or signs of a violation are detected and confirmed through specialised tools and technical equipment;\n(c) where a denunciation or a tip-off indicates a criminal or unlawful act;\n(d) where a competent authority requests the hot pursuit or detention of an offender, vessel or tool;\n(e) where the offender voluntarily confesses the unlawful act.\n3. When carrying out patrol, inspection and search, the vessels, aircraft and other means of the Vietnam Coast Guard must display their colours and, in accordance with the provisions of Articles 29 and 31 of this Law, display their flags, insignia, markings and uniforms.\n4. Agencies, organisations and individuals in the Vietnamese sea area shall be obliged to submit to inspection and search by the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n·101·\n5. The Minister of National Defence shall prescribe the procedures for patrol, inspection and search by the Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 13"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 14,
        "text": {
          "en": "Use of weapons, explosives and supporting tools\n1. In the performance of their tasks, officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard may use weapons, explosives and supporting tools and may open fire in accordance with the provisions of the Law on Management and Use of Weapons, Explosives and Supporting Tools.\n2. In addition to opening fire in accordance with the provisions of the Law on Management and Use of Weapons, Explosives and Supporting Tools, in the performance of the tasks of preventing and combating crimes and ensuring maritime peace, order and security, officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard may open fire at vessels other than vessels of diplomatic representative missions, vessels of foreign consular institutions, vessels of representative offices of international organisations, vessels carrying passengers or vessels carrying hostages which have already stopped, in any of the following circumstances:\n(a) where a vessel already under control attacks or directly threatens the life of a person performing official duties or of another person;\n(b) where a vessel clearly under the control of a criminal offender shows an intention to flee;\n(c) where it is clearly established that the vessel carries criminal offenders or carries unlawful weapons, explosives, reactionary materials, State secrets, narcotics or national treasures and shows an intention to flee;\n(d) where it is clearly known that persons on board commit acts of piracy or armed robbery of vessels as provided for in the international treaties to which the Socialist Republic of Vietnam is a party and in the Penal Code and that the vessel shows an intention to flee.\n3. In the cases of opening fire provided for in clause 2 of this Article, officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard must, before opening fire at a vessel, give a warning by an act, an order, words or a shot fired into the air; when carrying out an organised task, they must comply with the orders of the person with competence."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 14"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 15,
        "text": {
          "en": "Use of facilities and specialised technical equipment\n1. The Vietnam Coast Guard shall use facilities and specialised technical equipment and the results obtained thereby in accordance with the law on the protection of national security, the law on the handling of administrative violations and the law on criminal procedure when collecting, analysing, assessing and forecasting situations for the safeguarding of the sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction of the State and the protection of the maritime security and interests of the nation and the State, and when detecting, arresting, investigating and handling crimes and unlawful acts.\n2. The facilities and specialised technical equipment of the Vietnam Coast Guard must, before being put into use, be appraised, calibrated and tested so as to conform to the procedures prescribed by law and to ensure safety.\n3. The Government shall prescribe the management, use and list of facilities and specialised technical equipment used by the Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 15"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 16,
        "text": {
          "en": "Mobilisation of civilian persons, vessels, tools and technical equipment\n1. In cases where the urgent arrest of offending persons, vessels or tools is required, in search and rescue, and in responding to and overcoming serious environmental incidents, officers and soldiers of the Coast Guard may mobilise persons, vessels, tools and technical equipment belonging to Vietnamese agencies, organisations and citizens.\n2. The mobilisation provided for in clause 1 of this Article must ensure conformity with the actual capacity of the civilian persons, vessels, tools and technical equipment mobilised and must be followed by immediate return once the emergency situation has ended.\nWhere persons or property mobilised for participation in the work suffer harm, they shall enjoy the regimes, Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 1 ·102· policies and compensation provided for in clause 3 of Article 6 of this Law. The agency to which the officers and soldiers carrying out the mobilisation belong shall be responsible for paying compensation in accordance with the provisions of law.\n3. Vietnamese agencies, organisations and citizens shall be responsible for completing the mobilisation requests of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n4. In cases where the urgent arrest of offending persons, vessels or tools is required, in the conduct of search and rescue, and in responding to and overcoming serious environmental incidents, officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard may propose that foreign organisations and individuals operating in the Vietnamese sea area participate in assistance and rescue."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 16"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 17,
        "text": {
          "en": "Exercise of the right of hot pursuit of vessels at sea\n1. The Vietnam Coast Guard shall exercise the right of hot pursuit of vessels at sea in the following cases:\n(a) where the sovereignty, sovereign rights or jurisdiction of the Vietnamese State is violated at sea;\n(b) where the signals and orders to stop a vessel of the Coast Guard provided for in clause 3 of Article 13 of this Law are not complied with;\n(c) where international pursuit cooperation is carried out;\n(d) other cases provided for by law.\n2. The scope of, powers in and procedures for the exercise by the Vietnam Coast Guard of the right of hot pursuit of vessels at sea shall comply with the provisions of Vietnamese law and of the international treaties to which the Socialist Republic of Vietnam is a party."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 17"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 18,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Publication, notification and modification of navigational safety indices] The Command of the Vietnam Coast Guard shall be responsible for publishing or modifying navigational safety indices and notifying them to the competent authorities, for receiving and processing navigational safety information and for notifying vessels operating in the Vietnamese sea area of safe navigational methods appropriate to the needs of their application.\nPart 3 INTERNATIONAL COOPERATION OF THE VIETNAM COAST GUARD"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 18"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 19,
        "text": {
          "en": "Principles of international cooperation of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. International cooperation shall be carried out on the basis of compliance with Vietnamese law, the international treaties to which the Socialist Republic of Vietnam is a party and the international agreements relating to its competence, with respect for the basic principles of international law, so as to ensure the independence, sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction of the State and to safeguard the interests of the nation and the State and the lawful rights and interests of organisations and individuals carrying out activities at sea.\n2. Giving play to its own strength together with the support and assistance of the international community so as to ensure the enforcement of the law at sea."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 19"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 20,
        "text": {
          "en": "Content of international cooperation\n1. Prevention of and combat against piracy and armed robbery of vessels.\n2. Within the scope of the tasks and powers of the Vietnam Coast Guard, prevention of and combat against narcotics crime, trafficking in persons, unlawful arms trading, terrorist activities, unlawful entry and exit, unlawful cross-border transactions, unlawful transport of goods, unlawful exploitation of marine products and other unlawful activities at sea.\n3. Within the scope of the functions, tasks and powers of the Vietnam Coast Guard, prevention of pollution, prevention of, response to and handling of environmental incidents at sea, inspection for the protection of marine resources, protection of marine biodiversity and marine ecosystems, defence against and warning of natural disasters, humanitarian assistance, disaster response and search and rescue at sea.\n4. Conduct of training, fostering and professional drilling, exchange of experience, transfer of equipment and science and technology, and improvement of the capacity of the Vietnam Coast Guard Belt and Road Legal Research, Volume 1 ·103·.\n5. Other content of international cooperation provided for in Vietnamese law, the international treaties to which the Socialist Republic of Vietnam is a party and the international agreements relating to the functions, tasks and powers of the Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 20"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 21,
        "text": {
          "en": "Forms of international cooperation\n1. Exchange of information relating to maritime peace, order and security.\n2. Organisation of or participation in international conferences and seminars on maritime peace, order, security and law enforcement.\n3. Participation, in accordance with the provisions of law, in the conclusion of international agreements with the functional agencies of other countries and with international organisations.\n4. Coordination in patrol, inspection and search so as to ensure maritime peace, order, security and the implementation of the law.\n5. Participation in exercises and training, and conduct of courtesy receptions and visits in accordance with the maritime law of countries in the world and in the region.\n6. Working with the permanent bodies and key departments having links with the Socialist Republic of Vietnam as provided for in Vietnamese law, the international treaties to which the Socialist Republic of Vietnam is a party and international agreements.\n7. Other forms of international cooperation provided for in Vietnamese law, the international treaties to which the Socialist Republic of Vietnam is a party and international agreements.\nChapter IV JOINT ACTIVITIES OF THE VIETNAM COAST GUARD WITH FUNCTIONAL AGENCIES, ORGANISATIONS AND FORCES"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 21"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 22,
        "text": {
          "en": "Scope of coordination\n1. In accordance with the provisions of this Law and other provisions of the relevant laws, the Vietnam Coast Guard shall take the lead and shall coordinate with the agencies, organisations and forces under ministries and with other ministerial-level agencies and provincial People's Committees in performing the tasks and exercising the powers of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n2. Joint work between the Vietnam Coast Guard and the agencies under the Ministry of National Defence shall be prescribed by the Minister of National Defence."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 22"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 23,
        "text": {
          "en": "Principles of coordination\n1. Coordination shall be carried out on the basis of the functions of the agencies, organisations and forces under ministries, of ministerial-level agencies and of provincial People's Committees; it is prohibited to hinder the lawful activities of agencies, organisations and individuals at sea.\n2. The Vietnam Coast Guard and the agencies, organisations and forces under ministries, ministerial-level agencies and provincial People's Committees shall coordinate with one another in promptly completing tasks and shall assist one another in performing the functions, tasks and powers provided for by law.\n3. Centralised and unified chairing and mobilisation shall be ensured, and information relating to national defence, security and operational methods of agencies, organisations and forces shall be kept confidential in the course of coordination.\n4. It shall be ensured that the forms of coordination are flexible, specific and effective and are closely linked with the persons in charge of the chairing and coordinating agencies.\n5. Where, in the same sea area, an unlawful act relating to the functions, tasks and powers of several agencies, organisations or forces is detected, the agency, organisation or force that detects it first shall handle it in accordance with its competence as provided for by law. In cases and on matters falling outside its competence, it shall transfer the case file, the persons, the exhibits, the vessel and the tools to the agency, organisation or force with competence to chair the settlement; the receiving agency, organisation or force shall be responsible for notifying the transferring party of the results of the investigation and handling.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 1 ·104·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 23"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 24,
        "text": {
          "en": "Content of coordination\n1. Exchange of information and materials, and research on and drafting of legal normative documents.\n2. Safeguarding national security, sovereignty, sovereign rights and jurisdiction at sea.\n3. Protection of the marine environment and resources, of the property of the State, organisations and individuals at sea, and of the life, health, reputation and dignity of individuals carrying out lawful activities at sea.\n4. Conduct of patrol, inspection and search at sea to protect maritime peace, order and security; prevention, detection, stoppage and combat of unlawful acts; and combat against and defence against piracy and armed robbery of vessels.\n5. Prevention of natural disasters, conduct of search and rescue, and overcoming of and response to environmental incidents at sea.\n6. Training, drilling and fostering of officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard and dissemination, popularisation and provision of education on the law to the people.\n7. Conduct of international cooperation.\n8. Coordination in other related activities."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 24"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 25,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Responsibilities of ministers and deputy ministers of ministerial-level agencies and of chairmen of provincial People's Committees in coordinating with the Vietnam Coast Guard] Ministers and deputy ministers of ministerial-level agencies and chairmen of provincial People's Committees shall, within the scope of their tasks and powers, be responsible, in accordance with the regulations of the Government, for coordinating with the Minister of National Defence in carrying out activities relating to the Vietnam Coast Guard.\nChapter V ORGANISATION OF THE VIETNAM COAST GUARD"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 25"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 26,
        "text": {
          "en": "Organisational system of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. The organisational system of the Vietnam Coast Guard comprises:\n(a) the Command of the Vietnam Coast Guard;\n(b) the regional Coast Guard Commands and the units directly subordinate to the Command of the Vietnam Coast Guard;\n(c) grassroots units.\n2. The Government shall prescribe the details of clause 1 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 26"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 27,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Traditional day of the Vietnam Coast Guard] The 28th of August every year is the traditional day of the Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 27"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 28,
        "text": {
          "en": "[International designation] The international designation of the Vietnam Coast Guard is Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 28"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 29,
        "text": {
          "en": "Colours, flags, insignia and identification marks of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. The vessels, aircraft and other means of the Vietnam Coast Guard shall have their own distinctive colours, flags, insignia and identification marks. When performing their tasks, vessels must fly the national flag and the flag of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n2. The Government shall prescribe the details of clause 1 of this Article.\n·105·"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 29"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 30,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Seals of the Vietnam Coast Guard] In the performance of its functions, tasks and powers, the Vietnam Coast Guard shall use seals bearing the national emblem."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 30"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 31,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Uniforms of the Vietnam Coast Guard] The Government shall prescribe the service numbers, ranks, grades, insignia, service uniforms and ceremonial uniforms of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\nChapter VI POLICIES, REGIMES AND ASSURANCE OF ACTIVITIES OF THE VIETNAM COAST GUARD"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 31"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 32,
        "text": {
          "en": "Funding and material foundations of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. The State shall provide funding and material foundations, land, offices and project support for the activities of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n2. The State shall give priority to investing in modern equipment for the Vietnam Coast Guard and to applying advanced research and scientific achievements."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 32"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 33,
        "text": {
          "en": "Equipment of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. The Vietnam Coast Guard shall have vessels, aircraft and other means, weapons of all types, explosives, supporting tools and specialised technical equipment for the performance of its functions and tasks.\n2. The Minister of National Defence shall prescribe the details of clause 1 of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 33"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 34,
        "text": {
          "en": "Ranks, grades, posts, service regimes, policy regimes and rights of officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. In accordance with the provisions of the Law on Officers of the Vietnam People's Army, the Law on Professional Soldiers, the Law on National Defence Workers, the Law on Military Service and other relevant laws, the regimes and policies on appointment, promotion, demotion, deprivation of rank and grade, increase and reduction of salary, education, training, recruitment, service and discharge of officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard and the rights provided for in other regulations shall be implemented.\n2. While serving in the army, officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard shall be entitled to preferential policies appropriate to the nature of their tasks and to their special work as prescribed by the Government."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 34"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 35,
        "text": {
          "en": "Conditions and criteria for selection by the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. Vietnamese citizens aged 18 years or over, of either sex, of good political qualities and morals, in good health, of clear aspiration and willing to serve the Vietnam Coast Guard on a long-term basis.\n2. Possession of certificates and diplomas in a speciality and of skills consistent with the task requirements of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n3. The Ministry of National Defence shall make specific provisions on the details of this Article."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 35"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 36,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Education and training of officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard] Officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard may participate in education and training in politics, professional knowledge, law, foreign languages and other necessary knowledge relating to their tasks and powers, and the development of talents serving the Vietnam Coast Guard on a long-term basis shall be encouraged.\nChapter VII STATE MANAGEMENT OF THE VIETNAM COAST GUARD AND RESPONSIBILITIES OF MINISTRIES, MINISTERIAL-LEVEL AGENCIES AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 36"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 37,
        "text": {
          "en": "Content of State management of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. Promulgating and submitting documents and organising the implementation of legal documents relating to the Vietnam Coast Guard within the scope of competence.\nBelt and Road Legal Research, Volume 1 ·106·\n2. Organising and directing the activities of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n3. Educating and training officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n4. Implementing the policies and regimes applicable to the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n5. Inspecting and supervising, settling complaints and denunciations, conducting preliminary and final reviews, and commending and handling violations in the activities of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n6. Disseminating, popularising and providing education on the law.\n7. International cooperation of the Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 37"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 38,
        "text": {
          "en": "State responsibility for the management of the Vietnam Coast Guard\n1. The Government shall exercise unified State management of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n2. The Ministry of National Defence shall be responsible to the Government for the State management of the Vietnam Coast Guard.\n3. Ministers and heads of agencies at the same level shall, within the scope of their responsibilities, be obliged to coordinate with the Ministry of National Defence in the State management of the Vietnam Coast Guard."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 38"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 39,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Responsibilities of People's Committees at all levels and of the Government] People's Committees at all levels and the Government shall, within the scope of their responsibilities and powers, create conditions for the Vietnam Coast Guard to use local land funds for the construction of barracks, vessel berths, warehouses and wharves, and shall carry out the dissemination of, popularisation of and education on the law relating to the Vietnam Coast Guard;\nand shall implement the social housing policy for officers and soldiers of the Vietnam Coast Guard in accordance with the provisions of law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 39"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 40,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Responsibilities of the Vietnam Fatherland Front and its member organisations] The Vietnam Fatherland Front and its member organisations shall, within the scope of their respective tasks and powers, be responsible for coordinating with the relevant agencies and organisations in disseminating information on and mobilising the people to implement the law on the Vietnam Coast Guard and for supervising the Vietnam Coast Guard in accordance with the provisions of law.\nChapter VIII IMPLEMENTATION PROVISIONS"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 40"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 41,
        "text": {
          "en": "[Effect] The Law on the Vietnam Coast Guard shall take effect from 1 July 2019.\nThe Ordinance on the Vietnam Coast Guard Force No. 03/2008/PL-UBTVQH12 promulgated on 26 January 2008 shall cease to have effect from the date on which this Law takes effect.\nThis Law was adopted at the Sixth Session of the Fourteenth National Assembly of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 41"
        }
      }
    ]
  };

